window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["2c"]={"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/andorra":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"294dd1ae0ec9ffa954bee8c323c4834f0b6645be153bfeefdc5a0f225e81de1e","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/andorra","bytes":41804,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Andorra travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2012-12-13T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2025-12-10T12:46:46+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"p","s":["All travel to Andorra is through France or Spain. Check the ",["France travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/france"]," or ",["Spain travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain"]," before you travel for updates that may affect your journey."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/andorra/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://twitter.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/fcdotravel"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Andorra set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Andorra Ministry of Foreign Affairs","https://visitandorra.com/en/visitor-information/passports-visas-and-customs/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling through France or Spain"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must travel through France or Spain to enter Andorra. Check the entry requirements for ",["France","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/france/entry-requirements"]," or ",["Spain","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain/entry-requirements"]," before you travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To travel to Andorra, you must follow the ",["Schengen area passport requirements","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Andorra is not in the Schengen area, but you must travel through a Schengen area country to reach Andorra, either ",["France","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/france"]," or ",["Spain","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it may have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual Citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check visa requirements in ",["France travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/france/entry-requirements#visa-requirements"]," or ",["Spain travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain/entry-requirements#visa-requirements"],", as you’ll travel through one of those countries to reach Andorra. You must meet the requirements for entry to the Schengen Area in order to enter France or Spain for onward travel to Andorra, even if you have Andorran residency."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need a visa to enter Andorra."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Andorran Ministry of Foreign Affairs has further ",["information on entering Andorra","https://www.exteriors.ad/en/travel-to-andorra"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer than 3 months, you must go in person to the Andorran immigration department to ",["apply for a work or residence permit","https://www.immigracio.ad/ca/tramits-d-immigracio"]," (in Catalan)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling with children"]},{"k":"p","s":["Single parents or other adults travelling alone with children should bring a copy of any custody documents or written authorisation from the other parent."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Andorra guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/6/andorra#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the police"]},{"k":"p","s":["Hotels and tourist apartments must register the names of all overnight guests with the police. You must provide your passport details."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods that can be brought into and taken out of Andorra","https://visitandorra.com/en/visitor-information/before-you-arrive/passport-visas-customs/"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"p","s":["Andorra is not a member of the EU. You may have to pay taxes and duties on tobacco, liquor and luxury goods when you re-enter France and Spain."]},{"k":"p","s":["France and Spain have strict rules on the amount of tobacco exported from Andorra for personal use. Customs officers may impound excess amounts of tobacco and the vehicle transporting it."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Andorra"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Andorra, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Street crime is rare, but you should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["take care of your personal belongings"]},{"k":"li","s":["not keep money and valuables all in one place"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep a copy of your passport somewhere safe"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink and food spiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be aware of the possible use of ‘date rape’ and other drugs including GHB and liquid ecstasy. Buy your own drinks and always keep sight of them to make sure they are not spiked."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to drink alcohol in the street."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Andorra has a zero-tolerance policy on the possession and use of illegal drugs. Punishment for possession of even the smallest amount of illegal drugs can include refusal of entry, expulsion from the country, fines and prison sentences."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Andorra is a generally tolerant place for LGBT+ travellers. Same-sex marriage is legal in Andorra. Transgender people can register under their preferred sex in public documents without undergoing prior gender reassignment surgery."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Skiing"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to ski in Andorra, before you travel check:"]},{"k":"li","s":["weather conditions"]},{"k":"li","s":["safety advice, including protecting yourself against avalanches"]},{"k":"p","s":["For information, see:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["Ski Andorra","https://skiandorra.ad/en"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Visit Andorra","https://visitandorra.com/en/skiing/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["European Avalanche Warning Services","https://www.avalanches.org/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Preparing for winter sports abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/winter-sports-stay-safe-on-the-slopes"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Andorra, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Andorra guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/andorra/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Andorra. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"p","s":["Andorra has stricter drink-driving laws than many other countries, including the UK. You can get a fine of up to 600 euros and a 2-month driving ban. The Andorra authorities can also impound your vehicle."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check if you need a ",["UK sticker to drive your car outside the UK","https://www.gov.uk/displaying-number-plates/flags-identifiers-and-stickers"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["From 1 November to 15 May, you must use winter tyres or carry snow chains in Andorra. You can get a fine if you do not. Winter tyres have a snowflake icon on the wall of the tyre."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 116 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Andorra","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/6/andorra#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Andorra","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/6/andorra"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Andorra. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Andorra"]},{"k":"p","s":["‘Visit Andorra’ has a list of ",["medical centres and pharmacies","https://visitandorra.com/en/places-and-services/medical-centres-and-pharmacies-in-andorra/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Health Insurance Card (EHIC) and Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) are not valid in Andorra. Take out comprehensive travel and medical insurance before travelling."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Andorra"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 116"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 118"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 110"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mountain rescue: 112"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Andorra"]," in Andorra"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Andorra","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-information-pack-for-andorra"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["arrested abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/arrested-or-detained-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/andorra/british-consulate-general-barcelona"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/andorra/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://twitter.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/fcdotravel"]]}],"chars":10145,"change":"New information about dual nationals returning to the UK (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/politics-with-michelle-grattan-kos-samaras-and-richard-willingham-on-victorias-expected-change-of-government-296250":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"23166ed3e57bc9e21794738e830fba46605f1787fa60bf094a17048687046bef","read":"https://theconversation.com/politics-with-michelle-grattan-kos-samaras-and-richard-willingham-on-victorias-expected-change-of-government-296250","bytes":178462,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Politics with Michelle Grattan: Kos Samaras & Richard Willingham on Victoria’s expected change of government","by":[{"n":"Michelle Grattan","r":"Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra"}],"pub":"2026-11-11T00:06:20Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Victoria votes in just over two weeks, and although there’s still a long way to go, on present numbers the Liberals’ Jess Wilson, a first term MP, who leads a Coalition with the Nationals, is set to become premier."]},{"k":"p","s":["While the momentum is suggesting a majority government, there is still a question about whether the result could be a Coalition minority government that would have to rely on One Nation for confidence and supply."]},{"k":"p","s":["In our last podcast on the Victorian election, we spoke to the Premier Ben Carroll, Wilson and deputy One Nation leader, Colleen Harkin."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Politics with Michelle Grattan: why Victoria’s unpredictable election matters nationally","https://theconversation.com/politics-with-michelle-grattan-why-victorias-unpredictable-election-matters-nationally-293700"]]},{"k":"p","s":["This time we feature two close observers of the contest, pollster Kos Samaras from the RedBridge consultancy group and the ABC’s Victorian state reporter Richard Willingham."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a desperate late bid, Labor switched leaders from Jacinta Allan to Carroll. Has it helped? Samaras is clear:"]},{"k":"p","s":["Based on the numbers that we’re now seeing, and that some polls have already published and we’ll be publishing very soon, no. Objectively, Labor has structurally declined in terms of its presence in the polls and in terms of seat count; it’s probably worse off than it was under Jacinta Allan."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s pretty clear to me that the biggest impact of that leadership change wasn’t necessarily giving them an opportunity to distance themselves from the past. It was actually ruining their campaign infrastructure. So a lot of individuals who were working around Jacinta, who were part of that campaign machine, are no longer present. And that’s a fairly big impact. It takes a long time to build up speed into a campaign."]},{"k":"p","s":["On One Nation’s fortunes in Victoria, Samaras highlights it is not as well-placed as elsewhere:"]},{"k":"p","s":["At the beginning of this year in Victoria, for example, when we asked people why they were voting for One Nation, they said they didn’t think the Coalition could win, or was up to it. Both of those reservations have been removed off the table, and we can see the consequences of that writ large in the polling numbers."]},{"k":"p","s":["I would say New South Wales is going to be an absolute positive ballgame for One Nation. Victoria is a bit of an outlier. [There is] a deeply unpopular Labor government, a highly fragmented electorate, a Coalition that’s now deemed as the change agent. That is not the mix that we see in New South Wales."]},{"k":"p","s":["Willingham says Labor launching the royal commission into the “Big Build” during the election campaign could be the “death knell” for a Labor comeback:"]},{"k":"p","s":["Already on just the first day it laid bare some really big, interesting questions about the influence of the Labor government, its relationship with the CFMEU and the extra cost of dealing with the CMFEU imposed on taxpayers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ben Carroll has made calling this royal commission an absolute virtue. It’s the first major act he did since becoming premier. They’ve even put it in their campaign material."]},{"k":"p","s":["But this could be a very much own goal because it is going to drag [the] Labor government’s name through the mud and it’s going to expose a lot of allegations that have been made over the last four or five years about the Big Build and the processes that went to it."]},{"k":"p","s":["I think the fact that’s happening in the middle of the election campaign would probably just really cement in people’s minds that there were some serious issues going on under the Labor government and nothing was done about it."]},{"k":"p","s":["Willingham outlines what he sees as Wilson’s appeal:"]},{"k":"p","s":["I think a lot of people [are] getting behind Jess Wilson, who may not even have been Liberal voters in the past, because they want a change. […] Jess Wilson is very presentable, says the right things, she feels warm and open and different. She’s a new Liberal, she’s not part of the old guard that had leadership change after leadership change over the last 12 years. I think she presents something new and people are in the mood for change."]},{"k":"p","s":["Willingham sees few federal factors playing into the state campaign – except for One Nation voters:"]},{"k":"p","s":["A lot of voters […] have turned on the state government because of the state government’s performance. I don’t think it has anything to do with the prime minister or the federal Labor government’s performance. I think this is all about 12 years of the Andrews, Allan, Carroll governments."]},{"k":"p","s":["Samaras, meanwhile, says the recent interest rate rise has hurt Labor:"]},{"k":"p","s":["The interest rate rise on Melbourne Cup day […] I think has been quite impactful. People argue that while the Victorian Labor premier is not responsible for the inflationary crisis, unfortunately for him and probably every leader is that whether you’re a premier, a prime minister, or even the RBA governor you are all, as far as voters concerned, decision-makers."]},{"k":"p","s":["So it’s probably why we’re seeing a decline in the Labor’s primary vote this week after the interest rate rise in Victoria. So I think there is a definitely federal impact be it on the margins, but significant enough."]}],"chars":4949},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/dominican-republic":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"bd3c721443bc155c57941f553cf3c30f11271db4a2cfd02d7204803453a0cada","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/dominican-republic","bytes":66893,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Dominican Republic travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-25T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-07T16:02:53+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/dominican-republic/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the Dominican Republic’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in the Dominican Republic set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Embassy of the Dominican Republic in the UK","https://www.dominicanembassy.org.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Up until 31 December 2026, British passport holders can enter and leave the Dominican Republic for tourism purposes, as long as their passport remains valid for the duration of their visit. Rules may change after this date. Check the ",["Dominican Republic Ministry of Tourism website","https://www.godominicanrepublic.com/travel/entry-requirements/"]," for the latest information."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Checks at border control"]},{"k":"p","s":["You may be refused entry if you do not have proof of onward or return travel when you arrive in the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["Border officials will take your fingerprints and take a photograph of you before allowing you into the country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry and exit form"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must fill in an ",["entry and exit form before arriving in and leaving the Dominican Republic","https://eticket.migracion.gob.do/"],". This form includes your:"]},{"k":"li","s":["customs declaration"]},{"k":"li","s":["international boarding and disembarking form"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can fill in the form up to 7 days before you arrive in the country. You’ll get a QR code, which you may be asked to show at check-in, or when you arrive in or leave the country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit the Dominican Republic for tourism without a visa for 30 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer (to work or study, for business travel or for other reasons), you must meet the ",["Dominican Republic government’s entry requirements.","https://migracion.gob.do/en/"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Extending your visit"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can pay to extend your visit up to 120 days by contacting ",["the Dominican Republic Immigration Service","https://migracion.gob.do/en/servicio/stay-extension/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip, check the ",["vaccinations and certificates you need in TravelHealthPro’s Dominican Republic guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/67/dominican-republic#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Depending on your circumstances, this may include a yellow fever certificate."]},{"k":"h","s":["Children and young people"]},{"k":"p","s":["For children aged 17 and under travelling alone, parents should check travel requirements with the airline and with ",["Dominican Republic Immigration Service","https://migracion.gob.do/en/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of the Dominican Republic","https://www.aduanas.gob.do/de-interes/consultas/"]," (in Spanish). You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Dominican Republic"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in the Dominican Republic, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political demonstrations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Political demonstrations sometimes happen, although not usually near tourist areas. Take care not to get caught up in demonstrations or large gatherings and follow the instructions of the Dominican Republic authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["The crime rate in the Dominican Republic is high. Incidents range from opportunistic bag-snatching and pickpocketing to violent crime and carjacking."]},{"k":"p","s":["Stay alert when on the street or driving to the risk of drive-by robberies. Thieves on motorcycles snatch bags and valuables from pedestrians and sometimes reach through the windows of cars at red lights to steal belongings."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been incidents where foreigners have been mugged at gunpoint or knifepoint during the daytime while walking in residential districts."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attackers may be armed, even if you cannot see a weapon."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can take steps to reduce your personal risk by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoiding remote areas, particularly after dark"]},{"k":"li","s":["not wearing expensive jewellery or carrying large amounts of cash"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping smartphones and other electronic items out of sight"]},{"k":"li","s":["not leaving your bags or other possession on chairs or tables in restaurants and bars"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping car windows shut and doors locked"]},{"k":"li","s":["carrying your bags on the side away from the road"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re robbed, do not fight back or do anything that puts you at greater risk."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Bank card crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bank card cloning and identity theft are a risk in the Dominican Republic. Keep your payment card in view when paying or pay in cash."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Incidents of assault, rape and sexual aggression against foreigners have occurred, including at beach resorts. In some cases, hotel employees or fellow guests have been implicated."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can take steps to reduce your risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking alone at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not leave drinks unattended"]},{"k":"li","s":["be wary when dealing with strangers, and anyone offering rides or other invitations"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a victim of a sexual assault or other crime, report it immediately to ",["the British Embassy in Santo Domingo","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-santo-domingo"],". Consular staff can help:"]},{"k":"li","s":["report the incident with local authorities"]},{"k":"li","s":["explain the process"]},{"k":"li","s":["translate for you if there’s a language barrier"]},{"k":"p","s":["See FCDO’s guidance for ",["victims of rape and sexual assault abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-rape-and-sexual-assault-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must formally report a crime to the Dominican Republic authorities before you leave the country so they can investigate."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry copies of the photo page of your passport and entry stamp with you."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are severe penalties for all drug offences. Possession of even small quantities of any drug can lead to a fine and a prison sentence. Pack your own luggage and do not carry anything through customs for anyone else."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re arrested for drug offences your case can take several years to progress through the judicial process, during which you’re likely to be held in detention. If you’re given a prison sentence, you will serve the whole sentence in the Dominican Republic."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Attitudes towards the LGBT+ community are mostly conservative throughout the Caribbean. Although same-sex sexual relations are legal in the Dominican Republic, same-sex marriages are not legally recognised. Showing affection in public may result in unwanted and negative attention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Nearly all Dominican hotels welcome LGBT+ travellers, although you should check when you book."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"p","s":["Providers of recreational and adventure tourism may not meet UK safety standards. Check safety standards with the tour operators or providers and make sure you’re insured if you take part in activities like water sports, boat tours, quad biking or horse riding."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Lifeguards may not be present at swimming pools or on beaches. Safety and rescue equipment may not be available."]},{"k":"p","s":["The sea can be dangerous, especially during the hurricane season, which runs from June to November. Follow instructions about sea conditions and warning systems."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re planning to drive in the Dominican Republic, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in the Dominican Republic for up to 3 months. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["For longer visits, apply for a local driving licence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Dominican Republic has a high death rate from road traffic accidents. Road accidents happen often, especially during holiday periods like Christmas and Easter."]},{"k":"p","s":["Risks you may face include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["other drivers not following traffic laws, weaving from lane to lane and rarely signalling"]},{"k":"li","s":["vehicles being in poor condition"]},{"k":"li","s":["drunk driving"]},{"k":"p","s":["The quality of roads varies, although the majority of motorways connecting cities and tourist areas are in good condition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving outside the main cities at night can be dangerous because of poor lighting, animals or pedestrians on the road and cars driving without headlights."]},{"k":"p","s":["If police stop you for a traffic violation, they may issue you with a fine. They should not ask you for money directly. Pay the fine online on ",["Infracciones de transito","https://pgr.gob.do/servicios/consulta-y-pago-de-infracciones-de-transito/"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Motorbikes and scooters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take particular care if riding a motorbike or scooter. The Dominican Republic has a high death rate from road accidents involving motorbikes and scooters."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Traffic accidents"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in an accident, call the police and wait at the scene. If you’re detained because of a road accident, ask the police to contact the ",["British Embassy in Santo Domingo","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-santo-domingo"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drivers involved in an accident that causes serious injury or death will be taken into police custody until circumstances have been investigated. This will happen even if the driver appears not to be at fault."]},{"k":"p","s":["The telephone number for national roadside assistance is +1 829 689 1000."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Reporting an accident"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in a road accident, you must file a report with the Dominican Republic authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the accident did not cause injuries and happened in Santo Domingo or Santiago, you should register it at an accident reporting centre such as La Casa del Conductor or ",["El Centro del Automovilista","https://www.centrodelautomovilista.com/"]," (both in Spanish). These are government centres with English-speaking agents and representatives from the police and insurance companies."]},{"k":"p","s":["Talk to your insurance company for guidance on which centre you should use to report an accident."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the accident occurs in any other part of Dominican Republic, file a report at the nearest police station."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Taxis can be cheap but many are in poor condition. Use an authorised airport or hotel taxi service, or book from a registered provider. Ride-hailing apps are widely available."]},{"k":"p","s":["Motorbike taxis (‘motoconchos’) are often driven badly and do not always provide passengers with a helmet."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Public transport"]},{"k":"p","s":["Public buses and ‘carros publicos’ (shared cars that pick up passengers) are often uninsured, not registered and poorly maintained."]},{"k":"p","s":["Private companies operate good bus services between cities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hurricanes"]},{"k":"p","s":["The hurricane season in the Caribbean normally runs from June to November. Monitor local news and check the ",["World Meteorological Association weather reports for the Dominican Republic","https://worldweather.wmo.int/en/country.html?countryCode=DOM"]," and the ",["US National Hurricane Center’s regional map","https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Spanish language alerts are available from the Dominican Emergency Management Centre (COE) via their mobile app ‘AlertaCOE’, or their ",["X account","https://x.com/coe_rd"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hurricanes can cause localised flooding and damage to buildings. In recent years this has affected the north and east of the country."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes and tsunamis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes are a risk in the Dominican Republic. If an earthquake happens, follow the advice of the local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of tsunamis from earthquakes at sea. Follow the instructions of the local authorities if there are warnings of an approaching tsunami."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of the Dominican Republic. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/dominican-republic"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/dominican-republic/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dominican Republic-Haiti border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO ",["advises against all travel to Haiti","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/haiti"]," due to the volatile security situation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Land and sea borders between the Dominican Republic and Haiti are closed. Military and police roadblocks are common in the border area. The roadblocks can appear to be unofficial, although the soldiers do wear army uniform and carry weapons."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been armed robberies on roads close to the border with Haiti, including by criminals posing as police officers."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 911 and ask for an ambulance if you’re in Santo Domingo."]},{"k":"p","s":["The 911 emergency number does not have full coverage across the country. If you’re outside Santo Domingo, you can telephone the tourist police (‘Politur’) in an emergency: +1 809 200 3500."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for the Dominican Republic","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/67/dominican-republic#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in the Dominican Republic","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/67/dominican-republic"]," including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Malaria"]},{"k":"li","s":["Oropouche virus disease"]},{"k":"li","s":["Dengue"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of the Dominican Republic. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tap water quality"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tap water in the Dominican Republic is not drinkable."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some medicines normally only available on prescription in the UK are available in pharmacies across the Dominican Republic."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in the Dominican Republic"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in the Dominican Republic","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/dominican-republic-list-of-medical-facilities-and-practitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"p","s":["Public medical facilities in the Dominican Republic are generally limited. Private hospitals offer good standards of care, although they can be expensive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you have appropriate travel health insurance and can access funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation. In some cases, visitors who cannot pay their hospital bills are stopped from leaving the country until the debt is paid."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medical tourism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are risks associated with cosmetic or elective surgery overseas."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re considering travelling to the Dominican Republic for medical, surgical or dental treatment:"]},{"k":"li","s":["read advice on ",["going abroad for medical treatment and elective surgery","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad#going-abroad-for-medical-treatment-and-planned-elective-surgery"]]},{"k":"li","s":["discuss plans with your UK doctor before going ahead with any medical procedures abroad"]},{"k":"li","s":["research and verify the credentials and qualifications of any healthcare providers before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find further ",["advice on medical tourism from TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/59/medical-tourism-travelling-for-treatment"],", and NHS guidance on ",["going abroad for medical treatment","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/going-abroad-for-treatment/going-abroad-for-medical-treatment/"]," and on ",["cosmetic surgery abroad","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/cosmetic-procedures/advice/cosmetic-surgery-abroad/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in the Dominican Republic"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 911 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"p","s":["The 911 emergency number does not have full coverage across the country. If you’re outside Santo Domingo, you can telephone the tourist police in an emergency: +1 809 200 3500."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=dominican%20republic"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=dominican%20republic"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=dominican%20republic"]," in the Dominican Republic"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in the Dominican Republic","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/what-to-do-after-a-british-person-dies-in-the-dominican-republic"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in the Dominican Republic","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/dominican-republic-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/dominican-republic/british-embassy-santo-domingo"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/dominican-republic/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in the Dominican Republic","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-dominican-republic"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":18181,"change":"Updated information about outdoor activities and adventure tourism (‘Safety and security’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"2944661a7aa240b7c7bd089e0094692aacde4e8765fa307b27fcb2259f086a0f","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/spain","bytes":125687,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Spain travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-19T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-10T15:29:19+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["National strike"]},{"k":"p","s":["A national strike is planned for 11 November and is expected to affect transport systems. Check with your travel provider for the latest information."]},{"k":"h","s":["High speed rail collision in Toledo"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 28 October, two high-speed trains collided between the towns of Mora and Orgaz, in the province of Toledo."]},{"k":"p","s":["Significant disruption to rail services is expected."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need assistance or information about affected passengers, contact RENFE on +34 900 10 10 20."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a British national and you or a family member have been affected by the accident and need consular support, call the nearest British embassy or consulate on +34 91 714 6300."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to travel to Spain through France, check the ",["travel advice for France","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/france"]," before you start your journey."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to travel to Spain through Gibraltar, check the ",["travel advice for Gibraltar","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/gibraltar"]," before you start your journey."]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about the risks of travel, to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Spain set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Spanish Embassy in the UK","https://www.exteriores.gob.es/Embajadas/londres/en/Paginas/index.aspx"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Spain follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["travel without a visa to the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/travel-to-eu-schengen-area"],", which includes Spain, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The requirements for ",["working in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-spain-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day visa-free limit. Visits to Schengen countries in the 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days. If you overstay the 90-day visa-free limit, you may be banned from entering Schengen countries for up to 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are visiting Spain and need to extend your visa-free stay for exceptional reasons, such as a medical emergency, you must ",["apply to the immigration authorities (‘Extranjería’)","https://sede.policia.gob.es/portalCiudadano/_en/tramites_extranjeria_tramite_prorrogaestancia_cortaduracion.php#"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals living in Spain do not need their passports to be stamped. Actively show your proof of residence, such as the foreigner’s identity card (‘la tarjeta de identidad de extranjero’ or ‘TIE’), when presenting your passport at Spanish border control."]},{"k":"p","s":["At Spanish border control, you may need to show:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["proof of your travel insurance"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have enough money for your stay - the amount varies depending on your accommodation"]},{"k":"li","s":["a hotel booking confirmation or proof of address if you’re staying at your own property"]},{"k":"li","s":["an invitation or proof of address if staying with a third party, friends or family, such as a ‘carta de invitation’ completed by your hosts"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using the e-gates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Use the desks staffed by border officers. If you’re aged 18 or over, and airport staff instruct you to, you may be able to use e-gates when they are working."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you use an e-gate, make sure you get your passport stamped."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, you must ",["meet the Spanish government’s entry requirements","https://www.exteriores.gob.es/Consulados/londres/en/ServiciosConsulares/Paginas/Consular/Visado-de-trabajo-por-cuenta-ajena.aspx"],". If you’re in Spain with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more about ",["entry processes if you live in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-spain#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you cannot return to the UK before your visa-free limit expires, ",["contact the Spanish government’s national information service","https://sede.administracionespublicas.gob.es/pagina/index/directorio/ayuda_extranjeria/language/en"],". Dial 060 from a Spanish phoneline (in Spanish, with English speaking operators available)."]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling between Spain and Gibraltar"]},{"k":"p","s":["Following the provisional application of the ",["UK-EU Agreement in respect of Gibraltar","https://www.treaty.gov.gi/"]," on 15 July 2026 there are no longer entry and exit check booths at the land border for travellers between Gibraltar and Spain."]},{"k":"p","s":["Gibraltar’s entry conditions for short-stay visitors now formally aligns with Schengen requirements so you should ",["check the travel advice for entry requirements for Gibraltar","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/gibraltar"],", whether or not you’re planning to visit Spain before or after your trip."]},{"k":"h","s":["Unaccompanied minors"]},{"k":"p","s":["Spanish law considers anyone aged 17 and under to be a minor. If an unaccompanied minor comes to the attention of the Spanish authorities (particularly in connection with criminal incidents or when in hospital), they will be considered vulnerable and may be taken to a minor’s centre until a parent or guardian is found."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Spain guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/203/spain#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about what ",["goods can be taken into and out of Spain (PDF)","https://sede.agenciatributaria.gob.es/static_files/Sede/Tema/Viajeros_Desplazados/Viajeros/Brochure_Travellers_arriving_from_non_EU_countries.pdf"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food into Spain"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EU countries","https://food.ec.europa.eu/animals/animal-products-movements/personal-imports_en"],". There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Spain"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 euros or more. You will get a certified declaration to show you brought it in with you. If you do not, your money could be seized when you leave."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Spain"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorists are likely to try and carry out attacks in Spain."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["In January 2023, one person was killed and others injured in a machete attack at 2 churches in Cadiz"]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations, political gatherings or marches can take place with little or no warning, particularly in cities. Follow the advice of police and local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["While most demonstrations are peaceful, there is a risk of unrest or violence. If you’re near areas where demonstrations are taking place, be aware of what is happening around you and move away if there are signs of disorder."]},{"k":"h","s":["Lost or stolen passports"]},{"k":"p","s":["If your passport is lost or stolen, you must ",["renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," or ",["apply for an emergency travel document","https://www.gov.uk/travel-urgently-from-abroad-without-uk-passport"],". You cannot use a police report to leave Spain."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be alert to the risk of street crime. Thieves use distraction techniques and often work in teams. Take care of your passports, money and personal belongings, particularly when collecting or checking in luggage at the airport, and while arranging car hire."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not carry all your valuables in one place. Keep a copy of the photo page of your passport somewhere safe."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure your accommodation has adequate security. Keep all doors and windows locked. If you’re concerned about the security of your accommodation, speak to your travel operator or the property owner."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Spanish Police and Civil Guard have a mobile app which offers a number of services, including simple and discreet communication with police forces if you experience or witness a crime – more information can be found on the ",["ALERT COPS webpage","https://alertcops.ses.mir.es/publico/alertcops/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Vehicle crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["‘Highway pirates’ target foreign-registered and hire cars, especially those towing caravans. They may flag you down, claiming there is something wrong with your car or that you have damaged theirs. If you decide to stop to check the condition of a vehicle, stop in a public area with lights, such as a service station. Be wary of anyone offering help."]},{"k":"p","s":["When driving, be wary of approaches from people posing as plainclothes police in unmarked cars. In all traffic-related matters, police officers will usually be in uniform."]},{"k":"p","s":["Unmarked police vehicles have a flashing electronic sign on the rear window saying ‘Policía’ or ‘Guardia Civil’. Genuine police will only ask you to show vehicle documents – not to hand over your bag or wallet."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Attacks and sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks, including sexual assaults, are rare but do occur. In an emergency call 112."]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid splitting up from your friends, do not leave drinks unattended and do not go off with people you do not know. Save the location of your accommodation on your maps app, so it’s easy to find. ",["See TravelAware ‘Stick with your mates’","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/#stick_with_your_mates"]," for tips and advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["See advice on ",["getting help if you are a victim of crime abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":[["British Consulates in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-madrid"]," (+34 917 146 300 or +44 207 008 5000) can provide local information and support."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking and alcohol"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be alert to the possible use of ‘date rape’ and other drugs including GHB and liquid ecstasy."]},{"k":"p","s":["Buy your own drinks and always keep them in sight to avoid them being spiked. Alcohol and drugs can reduce your vigilance, making you less in control. If you drink, know your limit. Drinks served in bars in Spain are often stronger than those in the UK."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scams"]},{"k":"p","s":["Thieves posing as police officers may ask to see your wallet, claiming they need to see it for identification. Genuine police officers will ask to see ID but will not ask for wallets or purses. All police officers, including those in plain clothes, carry official ID."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Timeshares and holiday clubs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be cautious if you are planning to buy or sell a timeshare. There are companies who offer false incentives."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["timeshare fraud advice from Action Fraud","https://www.actionfraud.police.uk/a-z-of-fraud/timeshare-fraud"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must provide photo ID if asked by a police officer. This includes the Guardia Civil and national, regional and local police forces. The police have the right to hold you at a police station until they have confirmed your identity."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ignoring direct requests of a police officer can be considered as ‘disobedience’, which is a criminal offence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hotels, tourist accommodation and car rental companies have a legal duty to register passport and other details of tourists who check in or collect a vehicle."]},{"k":"p","s":["When checking in to your accommodation, wait until hotel staff have registered your passport details, or taken a copy of your passport. Do not leave your passport at reception to collect later."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may need to show ID when buying goods with credit or debit cards. Some shops will accept your driving licence or a copy of your passport, but some may ask for your passport."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws and bans"]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal to drink alcohol in the street in some areas of Spain. You can get an on-the-spot fine. There are strict controls on drinking and sexual activity in public places, including on beaches. Some areas also impose fines for the sale or consumption of nitrous oxide, also known as laughing gas."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws in the Balearic Islands"]},{"k":"p","s":["Local laws limit the sale and availability of alcohol in areas of some resorts on the Balearic Islands, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Magaluf (Calvià), Mallorca"]},{"k":"li","s":["Playa de Palma, Mallorca"]},{"k":"li","s":["San Antonio (San Antoni de Portmany), Ibiza"]},{"k":"p","s":["This bans:"]},{"k":"li","s":["happy hours"]},{"k":"li","s":["open bars (such as ‘all you can drink in one hour’ offers)"]},{"k":"li","s":["the sale of alcohol from vending machines"]},{"k":"li","s":["self-service alcohol dispensers"]},{"k":"li","s":["organising pub crawls and party boat trips"]},{"k":"li","s":["‘off-licence’ sales between 9:30pm and 8am"]},{"k":"p","s":["Hotels and other establishments will evict you if you behave dangerously on balconies. You could also get a fine."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Falls from height"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been a number of serious accidents, some fatal, involving British nationals falling from height, including from balconies."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not take unnecessary risks around balconies or other high places, particularly if you’re under the influence of drink or drugs. If you are staying in a room with a balcony, follow the safety advice at the hotel or apartment, and watch out for friends who may be at risk."]},{"k":"p","s":["In some regions you may be fined or evicted from your hotel if you are found to be behaving irresponsibly around balconies. Your travel insurance may not cover you for incidents that take place on a balcony or if you were under the influence of drink or drugs when the incident happened."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, carry severe penalties. You should expect a long jail sentence and heavy fines for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through airports. Possession of even a small quantity of drugs can lead to arrest and detention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Spain have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal commercial parties in villas and private homes"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been a number of serious accidents involving people attending illegal commercially promoted parties in villas and private homes on the islands of Ibiza and Mallorca."]},{"k":"p","s":["Licensed clubs and bars have to meet safety and security standards, including emergency exits and capacity limits, and have trained, licensed security staff. Illegal commercial parties may not meet these standards. Take care of your belongings, make sure you know where emergency exits are and do not take unnecessary risks."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may get a fine for attending illegal commercially promoted parties."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dress code"]},{"k":"p","s":["In some parts of Spain it’s illegal to be in the street wearing only a bikini or swimming shorts. Being bare-chested is also illegal in some areas in Spain. You could be fined if you’re caught wearing swimwear on the seafront promenade or adjacent streets."]},{"k":"p","s":["For security reasons, some public authorities in Spain do not allow the burka or niqab to be worn in their buildings. If you visit town council buildings wearing a burka or niqab, you may be asked to remove it while inside."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Changing money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always use official money exchange offices or banks because unofficial money changers may give you counterfeit money. Possession or use of counterfeit money is considered a serious crime in Spain and may lead to prosecution."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+"]},{"k":"p","s":["Spain is a generally tolerant and progressive place for LGBT+ travellers. There are active LGBT+ communities and social venues, particularly in big cities. Same-sex marriage is legal. Transgender people can register under their preferred sex in public documents such as birth certificates, identity cards and passports without undergoing prior gender reassignment surgery."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Festivals, concerts and processions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Many large events and public processions are held in Spain each year. Follow the advice of police and local authorities when attending events."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take care of your valuables if attending a festival or large concert. See the ",["festival travel checklist","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/festival-travel-checklist"]," for more advice."]},{"k":"h","s":["Swimming and beaches"]},{"k":"p","s":["Every year, people drown in the sea and in swimming pools in Spain."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming pools"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always supervise children. Keep small children within arms-reach in and around swimming pools, even if they can swim or there is a lifeguard present."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Beaches"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take care when swimming in the sea. Always supervise children and keep small children within arms-reach. Some beaches, especially around Spanish islands, may have strong undercurrents."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hidden rocks or shallow depths can cause serious injury or death. Do not dive into unknown water and do not swim at beaches where a river runs into the sea."]},{"k":"p","s":["Many beaches have a flag system. Make sure you understand the system and follow any warnings (a red flag means you must not enter the water). Take extra care and get local knowledge if there are no lifeguards, flags or signs."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are walking along unmonitored beaches, be aware that waves can come in further than expected and have strong undertows."]},{"k":"p","s":["See further tips on ",["ABTA’s swim safe pages","https://www.abta.com/tips-and-advice/staying-safe-on-holiday/swim-safe"]," and ",["Water Safety on Holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow local advice if jellyfish are present."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hill walking, skiing and mountain activities"]},{"k":"p","s":["Temperatures in some parts of Spain can change very quickly. Take extra care when planning a hike or walk. Check local weather reports for warnings of extreme heat or cold temperatures."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness may be a risk in some of the higher mountain ranges in Spain, particularly in the regions of Granada, Huesca and Tenerife. See ",["Health","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain/health"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For advice on safety and weather conditions for skiing or other outdoor activities you can:"]},{"k":"li","s":["call the Spanish National Tourist Office in London on 020 7317 2028"]},{"k":"li","s":["see the European Avalanche Warning Services"]},{"k":"p","s":["If an accident happens while mountaineering, canoeing, potholing or climbing, or if you become lost in the mountains and need mountain rescue, call:"]},{"k":"li","s":["112 for the emergency services"]},{"k":"li","s":["062 for the Civil Guard"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Catalonia region has started billing climbers, skiers and other adventurers whose negligent behaviour causes them to need rescue."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your airline, tour operator or transport company whether there is any planned strike action which might disrupt your journey."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Spain. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"],". Provisional licences are not valid for driving in Spain."]},{"k":"p","s":["To drive a car or a motorcycle over 125cc in Spain you must be 18 years of age or above, and at least 16 years old to ride a motorcycle up to 125cc. You must carry a valid driving licence and ID."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are using UK insurance, always carry your certificate with you in case you are stopped. Check that your UK insurance will cover you to drive in Spain, and for how long. This certificate is generally only valid for a stay of less than 3 months."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check the ",["requirements for driving if you live in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-spain#driving-in-spain"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving a British car abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["You may need a ",["UK sticker to drive your car outside the UK","https://www.gov.uk/displaying-number-plates/flags-identifiers-and-stickers"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving regulations"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Spain, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Spain guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/spain/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["Spain has strict drink-driving laws. Police regularly carry out roadside checks for alcohol and drugs. Penalties include fines, loss of licence and a prison sentence."]},{"k":"p","s":["The police can give you on-the-spot fines for a variety of driving offences including speeding. If you accept the fine and pay within 20 days, it will be reduced by 50%. More information on how to pay is available on the ",["Spanish driving agency","https://sedeclave.dgt.gob.es/IWPS5/help/sincertificado_en.html"]," website."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seatbelts are compulsory for all passengers in the front and back seats. Children under 1.35m in height must use an approved child safety seat secured on the back seat. Children are only permitted to travel in the front seat of the car if the rear seats are already occupied by other children or if the vehicle does not have rear seats."]},{"k":"p","s":["Car hire agencies can provide child seats, so let them know you need one when you reserve the car. Incorrect installation of a child seat or not wearing a seatbelt can be considered a serious offence and met with fines."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to use a mobile phone when driving, even if you have pulled over to the side of the road. You must be completely away from the road. Using an earpiece is also illegal. Only completely hands-free units are allowed."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Low emission zones"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some inner-city areas in Spain have introduced permanent low emission zones (‘Zonas de bajas emisiones’ – ZBE) where Spanish vehicles must meet specific exhaust emission standards and display an air pollution sticker. The stickers are not issued to vehicles with foreign registration plates, but some cities require you to register your vehicle in advance – for example, Barcelona."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Spanish government’s general information helpline 060 (if calling in Spain) or +34 902 887 060 (if calling from UK) may be able to provide further information."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Only use official registered or licensed taxis, or reputable transport companies you recognise. Licensing regulations differ across Spain and in certain cities pre-booking is required."]},{"k":"p","s":["Passengers caught using unlicensed taxi services are liable for fines of up to 600 euros. Make sure you book your taxi or airport transfer through a licensed firm."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Extreme temperatures"]},{"k":"p","s":["Extreme weather can affect many parts of Spain, particularly over the summer months. Check warnings from the ",["Spanish Meteorological Office (AEMET)","https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.aemet.es%2Fen%2Fportada&data=05%7C02%7CConsular.TravelAdviceTeam%40fcdo.gov.uk%7C7baec48b6c7649ab8d8008ded12a3f9b%7Cd3a2d0d37cc84f52bbf985bd43d94279%7C0%7C0%7C639178176540777518%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=5OZFaxUueDvef9bVfeEQFclpjnXpKtMdPMoPNs6Zjkg%3D&reserved=0"]," and the ",["European Meteorological Services","https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.meteoalarm.org%2Fen%2F%3Fs%3Ditaly&data=05%7C02%7CConsular.TravelAdviceTeam%40fcdo.gov.uk%7C7baec48b6c7649ab8d8008ded12a3f9b%7Cd3a2d0d37cc84f52bbf985bd43d94279%7C0%7C0%7C639178176540843608%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=PZU%2B%2FcMWUI8GZASi%2FYQptZwYmZ5jlVVUTtr38drLtuc%3D&reserved=0"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find advice on how to stay safe in hot weather from the ",["National Health Service (NHS).","https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nhs.uk%2Flive-well%2Fseasonal-health%2Fheatwave-how-to-cope-in-hot-weather%2F&data=05%7C02%7CConsular.TravelAdviceTeam%40fcdo.gov.uk%7C7baec48b6c7649ab8d8008ded12a3f9b%7Cd3a2d0d37cc84f52bbf985bd43d94279%7C0%7C0%7C639178176540900693%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=Wn3tOULr6WyaQz4I%2FgPLqcwR6%2FlI%2Ffh0CGI4dlUtac0%3D&reserved=0"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow guidance from the ",["Spanish Ministry of Health","https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sanidad.gob.es%2Fen%2FexcesoTemperaturas%2Fmeteosalud.do%23tituloBuscador&data=05%7C02%7CConsular.TravelAdviceTeam%40fcdo.gov.uk%7C7baec48b6c7649ab8d8008ded12a3f9b%7Cd3a2d0d37cc84f52bbf985bd43d94279%7C0%7C0%7C639178176540952683%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=g7Xh9kfrn6oheeSDsdAfVKCh%2FvJ6kgp45kUYXI8yHRM%3D&reserved=0"]," for your area. You can ",["sign up for email alerts","https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sanidad.gob.es%2Fen%2FexcesoTemperaturas%2FsuscripcionMeteoSalud.do&data=05%7C02%7CConsular.TravelAdviceTeam%40fcdo.gov.uk%7C7baec48b6c7649ab8d8008ded12a3f9b%7Cd3a2d0d37cc84f52bbf985bd43d94279%7C0%7C0%7C639178176541026260%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=gTLcEXJRvU%2BYp9kwP%2FytKNnVcfvohoj6hlNStaD0Dmc%3D&reserved=0"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider and follow advice from local authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Wildfires"]},{"k":"p","s":["Wildfires happen often in Spain, including on the islands, during the summer months."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be aware of your environment when visiting or driving through woodland areas. For information on wildfire risk visit the ",["Spanish Meteorological Office (AEMET)","https://www.aemet.es/en/eltiempo/prediccion/incendios"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Causing a wildfire is a criminal offence in Spain, even if unintentional. Make sure cigarette ends are properly extinguished, do not light barbecues and do not leave empty bottles behind. You can be heavily fined for not following the rules against lighting outdoor barbecues in forest areas. Make sure you know the rules if considering a barbeque."]},{"k":"p","s":["For information on what to do in the event of a wildfire, visit ",["Civil Protection","https://www.proteccioncivil.es/coordinacion/gestion-de-riesgos/incendios-forestales"]," (website in Spanish). Immediately report any fire you see to the emergency services on 112. In the case of wildfires, the situation can change quickly, so you should stay up to date with official advice."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["Flash flooding can cause travel disruption and damage to property and infrastructure. Check weather warnings from Spain’s meteorological office (AEMET) before travel and follow the advice of the local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Spain","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/203/spain#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Spain","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/203/spain"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["biting insects and ticks"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Spain. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries. There are details in the ",["guide to getting healthcare in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-for-uk-nationals-visiting-spain"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Spain"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/spain-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Spain, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is more ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re visiting Spain","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-for-uk-nationals-visiting-spain"],". There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-in-spain-including-the-balearic-and-canary-islands"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hospital treatment"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need hospital treatment in Spain, you’re more likely to receive appropriate care in public healthcare facilities. However, in some tourist areas there may not be any public healthcare facilities nearby. Your insurance or medical assistance company will be able to provide further details."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some hotels may call private doctors or ambulances to take patients to private hospitals. The GHIC or EHIC will not be accepted there."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Use of face masks"]},{"k":"p","s":["Due to an increase in respiratory infections such as flu and COVID-19, you may have to wear a face mask at doctors’ surgeries, hospitals and pharmacies. Rules on face masks may vary by region. Follow the advice of the local authorities and make sure you are aware of the measures in place."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Read FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Spain"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 112 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Reporting crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["To report a crime, including stolen property and lost or stolen passports, you can file a police report (‘denuncia’) at the nearest station of the:"]},{"k":"li","s":["National Police (‘Policia Nacional’)"]},{"k":"li","s":["regional police (‘Ertzaintza’ in the Basque Country, ‘Mossos d’Esquadra’ in Catalonia and ‘Policia Foral’ in Navarre)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Civil Guard (‘Guardia Civil’)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some Spanish cities also offer a ",["Foreign Tourist Assistance Service","https://www.policia.es/_es/dependencias_localizador_accesible.php"]," (‘Servicio de Atención al Turista Extranjero’ or ‘SATE’) where you can report crimes in English."]},{"k":"p","s":["While in Spain, you can ",["file a police report online","https://denuncias.policia.es/OVD/"]," for minor offences such as stolen bags or thefts from your car. If you’ve had belongings stolen, you may need to get a police report for insurance purposes."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to report a violent crime or sexual assault, you must go in person to the nearest police station."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Spain"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Spain"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Spain"]," in Spain"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/what-to-do-after-a-british-person-dies-in-spain"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/spain-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you are ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/spain/british-embassy-madrid"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/spain/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Spain","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-spain"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":31440,"change":"New information about a national strike planned for 11 November ('Warnings and insurance' page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ethiopia":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-23T16:51Z","sha":"8d649b41ba0a013384a9a318e4a52e4bdcb23efaf2b71ec49363be19dcf1fda2","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/ethiopia","bytes":114859,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Ethiopia travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-28T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-23T15:19:06+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Border areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within:"]},{"k":"li","s":["20km of the border with Sudan"]},{"k":"li","s":["10km of the border with South Sudan"]},{"k":"li","s":["100km of the Ethiopian border with Somalia and Kenya in Ethiopia’s Somali region, and within 30km of the Ethiopian border with Somalia in Fafan zone, except the border town and crossing at Wajale, and the principal road between Jijiga and Wajale"]},{"k":"li","s":["10km of the border with Kenya, except the Addis Ababa - Moyale road and Moyale, where we advise against all but essential travel."]},{"k":"li","s":["20km of the border with Eritrea"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tigray region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Tigray region."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Amhara region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Amhara region."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Afar region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within 20km of the border with Eritrea"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within 50km of the boundary with Tigray region."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the rest of Afar."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Gambela region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Gambela region."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Oromia region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 10km of the border with Kenya, except for principal roads and towns"]},{"k":"li","s":["north of (but not including) the A4 road in West Shewa zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["south and west of the A3 road in North Shewa zone, including the A3 road itself in North Shewa"]},{"k":"li","s":["West Wollega zone (including the main Addis Ababa to Gambela road), East Wollega zone, Kellem Wollega and Horo Gudru Wollega"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of East Shewa zone except the Addis Ababa to Adama Expressway, 10km either side of the Addis Ababa to Adaba expressway between Addis Ababa and Bishoftu, and the A7 from junction with the Addis Ababa to Adama expressway down to Hawassa"]},{"k":"li","s":["the A4 road between Addis Ababa and Cheliya, except for the section in the Finfine special zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["the remainder of North Shewa zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["West Guji and Guji zones (except the Hawassa to Mega road)"]},{"k":"li","s":["East Borena zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["Arsi zone"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Somali region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within:"]},{"k":"li","s":["30km of the Ethiopia-Somalia border in Fafan zone, except the border town and crossing at Wajale, and the principal road between Jijiga and Wajale"]},{"k":"li","s":["100km of the Ethiopian border with Somalia and Kenya in the rest of the region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Siti zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Nogob (previously Fik)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Jarar (previously Degehabur)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Shabelle (previously Gode)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Korahe and Dollo (previously Warder)"]},{"k":"li","s":["areas of Liben and Afder zones more than 100km from the Somalia and Kenya borders"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Central, Southern, Sidama and South West regions (formerly SNNPR)"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within 10km of the borders with South Sudan and Kenya including the shores of Lake Turkana."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Benishangul-Gumuz region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 20km of the border with Sudan"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Metekel zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Maokomo special zone"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the rest of Benishangul-Gumuz region."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about why ",["FCDO advises against travel to these regions","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ethiopia/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Conflict within northern Ethiopia"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is ongoing fighting between Ethiopian federal government forces and forces opposed to the federal government in northern Ethiopia. Tensions between Eritrea and Ethiopia are high and the security situation may change with little notice. Some flights are suspended and flights across northern Ethiopia may also be suspended or cancelled at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been some threats from armed groups against Alula Aba Nega Airport, Mekele."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are in the Tigray region, shelter in place. You should also:"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay indoors where possible"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow local restrictions, including curfews"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local media for developments"]},{"k":"li","s":["check with your airline for the latest information"]},{"k":"li","s":["as soon as you judge it is safe to do so, leave the Tigray region using commercial means"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["Staff at the British Embassy in Addis Ababa cannot offer support in person in areas of Ethiopia where FCDO advises against travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need urgent help from the UK government, for example if you’ve been arrested or you’re concerned about forced marriage, contact the ",["British Embassy in Addis Ababa","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-addis-ababa"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in the UK and are concerned about a British national in Ethiopia, call FCDO on 020 7008 5000."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ethiopia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Ethiopia’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Ethiopia set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how Ethiopia’s entry requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Ethiopian Embassy in the UK","https://www.ethioembassy.org.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your passport must be:"]},{"k":"li","s":["valid for at least 6 months after the date you arrive in Ethiopia"]},{"k":"li","s":["machine-readable"]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to visit Ethiopia for tourism or business."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need a visa if you have a valid Ethiopian Diplomatic ID Card, Ethiopian Temporary Residents Permit or Ethiopian Origin ID (Yellow Cards)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["We recommend UK nationals apply online for tourist and business visas before arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will need to provide a scanned colour copy of your passport and a colour passport sized photo. Get a 30-day single entry tourist visa from the official ",["Ethiopian e-visa platform","https://www.evisa.gov.et/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you visit Ethiopia on a tourist visa, you cannot take employment, including voluntary employment. If you break the rules, you can get heavy fines or be imprisoned."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Check what type of visa you need","https://www.evisa.gov.et/"]," for business, work, study or other reasons. Journalists will also need to obtain Media Arrival entry clearance via the ",["Arrival Clearance Portal","https://arrivalclearance.gov.et/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can apply for an official extension of 30 or 90 more days before your visa expires. You need to go in person to the Head Office of the Immigration and Citizenship Service in Addis Ababa."]},{"k":"p","s":["Leave the country before your visa or your extension expires. The penalty for overstaying is 30 US dollars a day. You will not be allowed to leave until you pay in full."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Ethiopia, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Ethiopia guide.","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/76/ethiopia#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"p","s":["You may need a yellow fever certificate for onward travel to some other countries, due to the risk of yellow fever in Ethiopia."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Mpox screening"]},{"k":"p","s":["Travellers arriving from some countries are required to complete an ",["Ethiopian Ministry of Health survey","https://cbs.ephi.gov.et/redcap/surveys/?s=M9RPX4HR9748PDCR"]," before arrival, if they are visiting Ethiopia or making a transit stop which includes movement outside of the airport. Check with your airline if the country you are traveling from requires this."]},{"k":"p","s":["You could be required to undergo testing or treatment if you have symptoms."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health screening"]},{"k":"p","s":["Travellers from some countries may be required to complete a health declaration form if they are visiting or transiting through Ethiopia. Check with the airline if the country you are travelling from requires this."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods that you can take into or out of Ethiopia."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to take in specialist equipment, such as filming or audio equipment, the Ethiopian Embassy in London advises you check with your tour operator or local contact before you travel as special customs regulations may apply."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact the ",["Ethiopian Embassy Press Office for more information and advice","https://www.ethioembassy.org.uk/public-diplomacy/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into and out of Ethiopia"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can carry up to 3,000 Ethiopian birr when entering or leaving Ethiopia. An exception is when travelling to Djibouti from Ethiopia, in which case you can carry up to 10,000 birr. If you are found with more than the legal limits, customs authorities can seize your cash and you could get a prison sentence."]},{"k":"p","s":["When you enter or leave Ethiopia, you must declare any cash over 10,000 US dollars (or equivalent in other currencies). Residents of Ethiopia, including resident British nationals, must declare any cash over 4,000 US dollars."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you leave Ethiopia with cash above these amounts, you must show officials the customs declaration form you got when you entered Ethiopia, or the bank advice note if you purchased currency locally."]},{"k":"p","s":["If entering Ethiopia through a land border you must declare cash worth over 500 US dollars."]},{"k":"p","s":["Keep your receipt if you change currency into birr. Without a receipt you are unlikely to be able to change leftover money back at the end of your trip."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Khat"]},{"k":"p","s":["Khat is legal in Ethiopia but it is illegal to take it out of the country. Customs officials regularly search luggage at Addis Ababa Bole Airport. Anyone found with khat is likely to be prosecuted."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Antiques"]},{"k":"p","s":["You need an export certificate to take antiques out of the country. If you do not have one, border officials are likely to confiscate your items if they find them. You could be arrested and prosecuted by the authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to buy or possess ivory – even your own pieces brought in with you. British nationals have had ivory jewellery confiscated and the authorities have imposed fines of up to 25,000 birr (about £220)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Satellite phones, binoculars and drones"]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal to import or export satellite phones, binoculars or drones to or from Ethiopia without prior approval from the Customs and Information Network and Security Agency. Get advice from your travel provider on permissions required."]},{"k":"p","s":["Unapproved items can be confiscated by authorities. You may be able to collect them on departure from Ethiopia, but this is not guaranteed."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Ethiopia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorists are very likely to try to carry out attacks in Ethiopia."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a threat of terrorism across Ethiopia. Attacks could target westerners, including British nationals. These could occur at any time including at religious events, public holidays or celebrations. Attacks could be indiscriminate and occur in places visited by foreign nationals, such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["restaurants, hotels, bars, nightclubs and markets"]},{"k":"li","s":["supermarkets and shopping centres"]},{"k":"li","s":["stations and airports"]},{"k":"li","s":["places of worship and tourist attractions"]},{"k":"li","s":["festivals and sports venues or events"]},{"k":"p","s":["Large crowds are common on key national and religious dates, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Ethiopian Christmas on 7 January"]},{"k":"li","s":["Epiphany or ‘Timket’ in January"]},{"k":"li","s":["Victory of Adawa on 2 March"]},{"k":"li","s":["Ethiopian Patriots’ Victory Day on 5 May"]},{"k":"li","s":["Downfall of the Derg Regime on 28 May"]},{"k":"li","s":["Ethiopian New Year from 11 to 12 September"]},{"k":"li","s":["Birthday of the Prophet Mohammed on 27 September"]},{"k":"li","s":["Finding of the True Cross or ‘Meskel’ on 28 September"]},{"k":"li","s":["Irreecha (Oromo thanksgiving festival) in early October (weekend of 3-4 October)."]},{"k":"li","s":["Ethiopian (Orthodox) Easter"]},{"k":"li","s":["Eid Al Fitr"]},{"k":"li","s":["Eid Al Arafa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Parts of the country also have local festivals (often celebrating saints’ days) which can lead to large gatherings."]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist group Al-Shabaab, based in Somalia, poses a threat across East Africa. The group threatens all countries who have military forces in Somalia, including Ethiopia. In 2022, Al-Shabaab attacked the Somali towns of Aato, Yeed and Washaaqo, near the border with Ethiopia. Al-Shabaab has said it intends to commit further attacks in the area."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Conflict and violence"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been reports of attacks, including possible drone attacks, on military installations in several locations across Ethiopia, including Addis Ababa. Further attacks are possible. Do not approach debris resulting from military activity, or touch unusual objects. Stay away from military sites and installations, and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Unrest can start with no notice. There are ongoing armed clashes between ethnic groups and with security forces in much of the country. Foreigners have not been targeted, but can get caught up in events. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local media"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow the advice of local authorities and your tour operator"]},{"k":"li","s":["reconsider travel to affected areas"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay away from large gatherings and demonstrations"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay away from military sites and installations"]},{"k":"p","s":["The security situation in northern Ethiopia may deteriorate quickly. There have been renewed clashes between insurgent groups and the Ethiopian military in parts of Afar, and Tigray, including reports of drone strikes which have increased in frequency since August 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["Since spring 2023 there has been significant conflict in Amhara region, between Amhara militia groups (Fano) and the Ethiopian authorities, as well as opportunistic crime and banditry."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been increased tensions and violence between armed groups and the security forces across Oromia region."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Strikes and protests"]},{"k":"p","s":["Civil unrest, including protests and strikes, may happen with little or no notice. This can cause:"]},{"k":"li","s":["temporary closures of roads, internet and mobile networks"]},{"k":"li","s":["disruption to local businesses and transport"]},{"k":"p","s":["In the past some protests have escalated into serious violence."]},{"k":"h","s":["Addis Ababa security checks"]},{"k":"p","s":["Travellers may face increased scrutiny, particularly if they are of Amhara, Tigrayan or Oromo ethnicity. Officials may carry out direct police operations including road checkpoints and door-to-door calls. Security services sometimes conduct house searches without authorisation."]},{"k":"p","s":["If security officials request access to your accommodation, check the credentials of the officials and then comply with their requests. The authorities have increased security checks at Bole International Airport and there are reports of ethnic Tigrayans being prevented from boarding flights. In all cases, avoid confrontation and follow the instructions of the authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk of arbitrary detention"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been a small number of cases of arbitrary detention of British nationals in Ethiopia. The risk may increase if tensions are heightened around major events, or in locations that are sensitive for security reasons. Carry a paper copy of your passport and contact details of the British Embassy in Addis Ababa (phone: +251 11 617 0100) in case you are questioned or detained."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Ethiopian authorities do not always notify embassies when they detain foreign nationals. If you are detained, ask family or friends to contact the British Embassy. The authorities may not permit embassy officials adequate access to detainees."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mugging (violent theft or theft with the threat of violence) is on the rise. There have been a number of incidents of sexual harassment of women."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take care of your belongings when visiting crowded public places, especially at night. There is a risk of petty theft around Bole International Airport, particularly pickpockets and bag-snatchers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be vigilant if strangers approach you. Criminal groups, often including children and young people, sometimes attempt to steal phones, wallets or other valuables by jostling and pickpocketing. They may use distraction techniques including begging, spitting, spilling food and drink, and feigning illness."]},{"k":"p","s":["There has been an increase in reported crime against foreign nationals and Ethiopian nationals in these areas of Addis Ababa:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Bole Medhanealem"]},{"k":"li","s":["Bole Atlas"]},{"k":"li","s":["Meskel Square"]},{"k":"li","s":["Yeka Hills"]},{"k":"li","s":["Entoto"]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks have included knifepoint robberies and victims being choked unconscious. Do not travel alone in these areas if possible. Be cautious if travelling on foot. Consider using road transport where possible, especially after dark."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Vehicle crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of bag and jewellery snatching from vehicles at traffic lights and of thefts from parked cars."]},{"k":"p","s":["When travelling in vehicles:"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep belongings with you – do not leave them in vehicles"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep valuables out of sight"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep windows closed and vehicle doors locked"]},{"k":"li","s":["park in well-lit and guarded areas such as hotels, government and tourist locations, which all have security at their entrances"]},{"k":"li","s":["consider fitting anti-shatter film to vehicle windows for longer-term stays"]},{"k":"p","s":["If threatened, hand over valuables without resistance."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal kidnap"]},{"k":"p","s":["Kidnapping is widespread in parts of Ethiopia, with incidents in the Oromia, Amhara and Somali regions and on the southern border with Kenya."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been a number of recent kidnappings in Amhara and Oromia, especially within the Adama special zone south of Addis Ababa, and western areas of Oromia region, particularly West Wollega, East Wollega and Qellem Wollega zones."]},{"k":"p","s":["Possible targets for criminal kidnap include people engaged in humanitarian aid work, journalism or business sectors. The motivation for kidnappings is extortion, with criminals demanding high sums for ransom."]},{"k":"p","s":["When arranging meetings, use a secure location. Avoid regular patterns of travel and aim to only travel during daylight hours. If you go to areas where the FCDO advises against travel, you will be at extreme risk."]},{"k":"h","s":["Landmines and unexploded bombs"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a continued risk of landmines and other unexploded ordinances following the end of the 2020-2022 northern Ethiopia conflict."]},{"k":"p","s":["The main areas of risk are:"]},{"k":"li","s":["along the internal border between Tigray and Amhara regions."]},{"k":"li","s":["along the international border with Eritrea"]},{"k":"li","s":["the rural regions and mountains in the south west of Tigray"]},{"k":"p","s":["Most incidents involve farmers in rural areas but be cautious and avoid traveling off the main roads in the affected areas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry a copy of the photo page of your passport and your visa and entry stamp. It is not a legal requirement to carry the original."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Religious customs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ethiopia is religiously diverse and largely tolerant. However, many people are devout in their faith. Take care to not offend local traditions, customs, laws and religions. Dress conservatively and observe local customs. Take particular care if travelling:"]},{"k":"li","s":["during the Islamic holy month of Ramadan"]},{"k":"li","s":["to religious sites of the Ethiopian Orthodox Church"]},{"k":"li","s":["outside Addis Ababa, particularly in rural areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ramadan is individually observed but there are no official regulations imposed."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health and safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Health and safety precautions like life jackets in boats or protective railings at historical sites are rarely in place in Ethiopia."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Date and time"]},{"k":"p","s":["Traditionally Ethiopia has a different calendar and way of expressing time than are widely used internationally."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most documents issued by hotels and larger organisations, including airline tickets, use the international calendar and clock. However:"]},{"k":"li","s":["official documents are often dated using the Ethiopian calendar"]},{"k":"li","s":["individuals and smaller organisations might use the Ethiopian clock"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ethiopia is a cash-based society. Banking facilities, including card payments and ATMs are limited, and unreliable, especially outside of cities. Make sure you have enough cash before leaving cities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drug offences are treated seriously in Ethiopia. Possession of drugs can lead to prison sentences of 5 to 10 years and potentially large fines."]},{"k":"p","s":["Khat is legal in Ethiopia, but it is illegal to take it out of the country. If you’re found with it in your baggage, the authorities are likely to prosecute you."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are no restrictions on buying or possessing alcohol. This includes the predominantly Muslim regions although it may be more difficult to obtain."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dress code"]},{"k":"p","s":["Women are expected to dress conservatively, especially when visiting religious sites."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex acts (applying to both sexes) are illegal and carry penalties of between 1 and 15 years in prison. Local authorities intimidate and detain owners and clients of hotels, bars and restaurants in Addis Ababa where same-sex activity is alleged to take place."]},{"k":"p","s":["The public are not generally accepting of same-sex relationships. Some religious leaders have called for violence against the LGBT+ community. Showing affection in public may receive unwanted attention. It is dangerous to be openly (or covertly) LGBT+ in Ethiopia, and can lead to great personal risk."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras or drones in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to take photos:"]},{"k":"li","s":["near military zones or of military personnel"]},{"k":"li","s":["near the Presidential Palace in Addis Ababa"]},{"k":"li","s":["anywhere that there are signs prohibiting photography"]},{"k":"p","s":["The use of drones is subject to permission from the Ethiopian Civil Aviation Authority and Information Network and Security Agency."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Fuel shortages"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fuel availability is currently limited across Ethiopia, with long queues and shortages at petrol stations reported nationwide. This may cause transport disruption and delays, particularly for travel outside Addis Ababa. If you are planning to travel within Ethiopia, check arrangements carefully with your tour operator or transport provider and allow extra time for journeys."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Ethiopia, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use your UK driving licence to drive for up to 2 weeks. To drive for longer, contact the Ethiopian Ministry of Transport and Logistics to apply for an Ethiopian driving licence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving standards and vehicle maintenance are often poor and there are frequent traffic accidents, especially in Addis Ababa and on the Addis Ababa-Djibouti road."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drivers involved in car accidents can face severe punishments, including custodial sentences and fines. If you’re involved in a traffic accident, stay in your vehicle and call the local police. Avoid confrontation and wait for police to arrive to deal with any issues."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel from the airport and around Addis Ababa"]},{"k":"p","s":["GPS signal is unreliable within Addis Ababa and nearby areas. You can download maps for use offline, and to familiarise yourself with routes before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Only use buses or taxis from the airport that have been organised by your hotel or travel company. If you are booking your own taxi, choose yellow or app-based taxis rather than the blue and white ones."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Transport outside Addis Ababa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid driving after dark anywhere outside Addis Ababa. Vehicles often have no lights and livestock may stray onto the roads."]},{"k":"p","s":["Mobile networks are not consistent or reliable. The authorities may close down networks with no notice. You should consider travelling in convoy with at least one other vehicle."]},{"k":"p","s":["Carry food and water, and ensure your vehicle is properly prepared and equipped. Medical facilities outside the capital are extremely limited so carry a comprehensive medical pack."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local demonstrations have led to temporary road closures, although not all social media reports of such disruptions are accurate. If unsure, contact local authorities or reputable tour operators in the areas you’re travelling to."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you come to a roadblock, follow advice of police or army personnel if they are present. If you encounter an unattended roadblock, turn around and do not attempt to pass it."]},{"k":"p","s":["Always leave details of your travel itinerary with a reliable person."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Public buses"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been instances where buses from one region have been attacked when passing through another."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of earthquakes and earth tremors. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["familiarise yourself with safety procedures in the event of an earthquake"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow advice given by the local authorities"]},{"k":"li","s":["call the Ethiopian emergency services on 939 if you are in immediate danger (it is likely they will answer in Amharic)"]},{"k":"p","s":["The US Federal Emergency Management Agency has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Ethiopia. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ethiopia/warnings-and-insurance"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ethiopia/safety-and-security"]," for Ethiopia."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel Restrictions"]},{"k":"p","s":["All civilian flights to and from Tigray region have been suspended. Foreign nationals are currently unable to travel to airports in Afar regional state due to restrictions by the Ethiopian authorities. Check with your operator before travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Ethiopian authorities may implement travel restrictions anywhere in the country at short notice."]},{"k":"h","s":["Border areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within:"]},{"k":"li","s":["20km of the border with Sudan"]},{"k":"li","s":["10km of the border with South Sudan"]},{"k":"li","s":["100km of the Ethiopian border with Somalia and Kenya in Ethiopia’s Somali region, and within 30km of the Ethiopian border with Somalia in Fafan zone, except the border town and crossing at Wajale, and the principal road between Jijiga and Wajale"]},{"k":"li","s":["10km of the border with Kenya, except for principal roads and towns"]},{"k":"li","s":["20km of the border with Eritrea"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Areas between 10-15km from the border with Eritrea"]},{"k":"h","s":["Tigray region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Tigray region."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is ongoing fighting between Ethiopian federal government forces and forces opposed to the federal government in the Tigray region. Some flights are suspended and flights across northern Ethiopia may also be suspended or cancelled at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are in the Tigray region, shelter in place. You should also:"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay indoors where possible"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow local restrictions, including curfews"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local media for developments"]},{"k":"li","s":["check with your airline for the latest information"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been some threats from armed groups against Alula Aba Nega Airport, Mekele. As soon as you judge it is safe to do so, leave the Tigray region using commercial means."]},{"k":"h","s":["Amhara region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to all of Amhara regional state."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been violent protests and clashes between security forces in Amhara since April 2023, resulting in an unknown number of deaths including of 11 aid workers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Violence has increased due to conflict between local militia groups (Fano) and the security forces. Since spring 2023 there has been significant conflict in Amhara region, between Amhara militia groups (Fano) and the Ethiopian authorities, as well as opportunistic crime and banditry. Movement is heavily restricted, arbitrary detention is a risk and the security situation is very volatile."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been reports of incidents along the Addis Ababa to Bahir Dar (A3) road, including armed men attacking vehicles. Attacks usually take place in the early hours when visibility is poor. Be highly cautious and avoid travelling when visibility is poor. The A3 road can be subject to increased checkpoints and closure by the Ethiopian National Defense Force (ENDF)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some flights across northern Ethiopia could be suspended or cancelled at short notice. Domestic and international land borders may be closed. Mobile networks and internet connections may be disrupted. You should monitor local media for further information and comply with local authority instructions."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been sporadic, violent clashes between armed groups in Amhara and in areas bordering Oromia region, including in urban centres. As these are not contained to specific areas, be cautious when travelling throughout Amhara. Situations can escalate quickly."]},{"k":"p","s":["Political disputes in the districts (‘woredas’) of Tsegede, Mirab Armacho and Tach Armacho have previously turned violent. Clashes have occurred around Chilga Woreda and Central Gondar zone, with reports of fatalities including civilians. While attacks are mostly related to inter-ethnic disputes and foreigners have not been targeted, attacks can occur at any time with significant risk of being caught up in violence. If you’re travelling in the area, monitor local media and follow the advice of local authorities and your tour operator."]},{"k":"p","s":["There has been an increase in roadblocks across Amhara region."]},{"k":"h","s":["Afar region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the parts of Afar region within 20km of the border with Eritrea and within 50km of the Tigray region, and advises against all but essential travel to the rest of Afar."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are irregular armed clashes between ethnic groups in Afar region and Somali Regional State, in particular along the main Addis Ababa to Djibouti road (A1) and the surrounding areas. Attacks are sporadic and infrequent, but may involve roadblocks and vehicles being targeted."]},{"k":"h","s":["Gambela region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Gambela region."]},{"k":"p","s":["Large-scale violence, inter-communal clashes and armed attacks occur regularly. While foreigners have not been targeted, there’s a significant risk of being caught up in violence."]},{"k":"h","s":["Oromia region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 10km of the border with Kenya, except for principal roads and towns"]},{"k":"li","s":["north of (but not including) the A4 road in West Shewa zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["south and west of the A3 road in North Shewa zone including the A3 road itself in North Shewa"]},{"k":"li","s":["West Wollega zone (including the main Addis Ababa to Gambella road), East Wollega zone, Kellem Wollega and Horo Gudru Wollega"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of East Shewa zone except the Addis Ababa to Adama Expressway and 10km either side of the expressway between Addis Ababa and Bishoftu and the Addis Ababa to Hawassa Expressway"]},{"k":"li","s":["the A4 road between Addis Ababa and Cheliya, except for the section in the Finfine special zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["the remainder of North Shewa zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["West Guji and Guji zones (except the Hawassa to Mega road)"]},{"k":"li","s":["East Borena zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["Arsi zone"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Increased tensions and armed groups"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been reports of armed clashes between regional security forces from Oromia and Somali regions in the area of the border between the two regions, including in Moyale town in October 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["Violence has increased due to conflict in Oromia, and there may be disruption to travel, and to the operation of businesses and services."]},{"k":"p","s":["In West and North Shewa zone there have been reports of attacks on vehicles travelling on the A1 road, between Adama and Awash, though not targeting international travellers."]},{"k":"p","s":["There were reports in October 2023 of air strikes against armed groups in West Shewa. These could take place in a range of locations without notice. You should be extremely cautious when travelling in the West Shewa zone, particularly in rural areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been repeated and serious instances of violence in West Wollega, East Wollega, Kellem Wollega and Horo Gudru Wollega. This has included clashes between armed groups and security forces, violent crime and roadside attacks."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are increased tensions and violence between armed groups and the security forces across Oromia region, particularly Shewa, Haraghe and Guji zones. Avoid military, police and security installations. Be extremely cautious and follow advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Since late 2022, there has been an increase in incidents between armed groups in Oromia. These have mainly been reported in rural areas and areas bordering Amhara region. Incidents are not contained to specific areas. Be cautious when travelling, particularly between urban centres. The situation can escalate quickly."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some protests have turned violent and resulted in casualties. There has also been severe disruption to road travel including major roads to and from Addis Ababa."]},{"k":"p","s":["Protests and demonstrations occur periodically in towns across Oromia region. Towns in the Wollega and Shewa zones, and West Arsi zone, including Ambo, Wolissa, Nekemte and Shashemene, have been particularly affected. Demonstrations have also taken place elsewhere including in the Bale zone to the south-east."]},{"k":"p","s":["In some instances, international investors have been threatened, although the British Embassy is not aware of foreign tourists having been targeted."]},{"k":"h","s":["Somali region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within:"]},{"k":"li","s":["30km of the Ethiopia-Somalia border in Fafan zone, except the border town and crossing at Wajale, and the principal road between Jijiga and Wajale"]},{"k":"li","s":["100km of the Ethiopian border with Somalia and Kenya in the rest of the region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Siti zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Nogob (previously Fik)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Jarar (previously Degehabur)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Shabelle (previously Gode)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Korahe and Dollo (previously Warder)"]},{"k":"li","s":["areas of Liben and Afder zones more than 100km from the Somalia and Kenya borders"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been reports of armed clashes between regional security forces from Oromia and Somali regions in the area of the border between the two regions, including in Moyale town in October 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are irregular armed clashes between ethnic groups in Afar Region and Somali Regional State, in particular along the main Addis Ababa to Djibouti road (A1) and the surrounding areas. Attacks are sporadic and infrequent, but may involve roadblocks with vehicles being targeted."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is local instability, lawlessness, military activity and a general risk of banditry in Somali region. Insurgent groups, some affiliated with terrorist organisations, have clashed with government forces. Foreigners have been caught up in the violence or targeted. There have also been attacks on staff working for international non-governmental organisations."]},{"k":"h","s":["Central, Southern, Sidama and South West regions (formerly SNNPR)"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within 10km of the borders with South Sudan and Kenya including the shores of Lake Turkana."]},{"k":"p","s":["Due to recent regional cluster changes, there have been instances of civil unrest as zones increasingly demand their own regional statehood. There has been civil unrest in Gurage zone in the newly formed Central region. A number of incidents have turned violent."]},{"k":"h","s":["Benishangul-Gumuz region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 20km of the border with Sudan"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Metekel zone"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Maokomo special zone"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the rest of Benishangul-Gumuz region."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is risk of armed clashes at near the border with Sudan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local tensions have led to frequent and unpredictable violent clashes, including reported abductions and deaths. There have been instances of civil unrest in and around Assosa."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 907 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Ethiopia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/76/ethiopia#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Ethiopia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/76/ethiopia"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["Malaria and dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["Schistosomiasis"]},{"k":"li","s":["Zika virus"]},{"k":"li","s":["Mpox"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 26 January 2026, the Ethiopian government and WHO officially declared the end of the Marburg virus disease outbreak. This announcement came after no new confirmed cases were reported for consecutive 42 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["Malaria is common in areas of the country up to 2,000 metres above sea level. Addis Ababa sits at 2,400m but a number of sites popular with tourists are below 2,000m."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bilharzia is present in the vast majority of lakes in Ethiopia – check before swimming."]},{"k":"p","s":["Water-borne diseases are common. Drink or use only boiled or bottled water and avoid ice in drinks."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is an ongoing outbreak of cholera in the south of Ethiopia, and new cases have been reported in the north-west of the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Ethiopia, including Addis Ababa. ",["TravelHealthPro’s altitude sickness guidance","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"]," has more information."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Ethiopia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Only private hospitals in Addis Ababa offer a reasonable standard of basic care for minor health problems. Dentistry is especially scarce. Ambulance services are also limited. Outside the capital, medical facilities are extremely poor. Carry a comprehensive medical pack when travelling out of Addis Ababa."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Ethiopia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ethiopia-doctors-and-medical-facilities"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Ethiopia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police and emergency: 911"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance (Addis Ababa): 907"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Ethiopia"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Ethiopia"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Ethiopia"]," in Ethiopia"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Ethiopia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ethiopia-bereavement-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Ethiopia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ethiopia-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/ethiopia/british-embassy-addis-ababa"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"p","s":["The current political and security situation means the British Embassy in Addis Ababa may only be able to provide limited support in person."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ethiopia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Ethiopia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-ethiopia"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":38837,"change":"FCDO now advises against all but essential travel to East Borena and Arsi in Oromia Region. FCDO continues to advise against all travel and all but essential travel to other parts of Ethiopia (‘Warnings and insurance’ and ‘Regional risks’ pages)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"eea884569d04f57bb75541181e8aea58b3ad18e9af61b98dafcfaba4702eae08","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala","bytes":81967,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Guatemala travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-13T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-16T16:17:57+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against all but essential travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 5km of the Mexican border from the Pacific Coast up to and including the Gracias a Dios crossing"]},{"k":"li","s":["to the towns of Santa Ana Huista, San Antonio Huista and La Democracia in the department of Huehuetenango"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["why FCDO advises against travel to these areas","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["State of Emergency"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Guatemalan government has declared a 30-day state of emergency in the departments of Zacapa and Izabal. Restrictions on movement, public gatherings and demonstrations may be imposed. Follow the instructions of local authorities and refer to official Guatemalan government sources for details of any restrictions in place."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Guatemala’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Guatemala set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Guatemalan Embassy in the UK","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/foreign-embassies-in-the-uk"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Guatemala, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive and at least 2 blank pages."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit Guatemala for up to 90 days without a visa."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you overstay the 90 days, you’ll have to pay a fine before leaving the country. You can pay this fine at the main Institute of Migration (in Spanish) office in Guatemala City. You can also pay fines at the airport or land borders, but administrative processes can cause serious delays."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for or extending a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer than 90 days (to work or study, for business, travel or for other reasons), you must meet the Guatemalan government’s entry requirements. Check which type of visa or work permit you need with the ",["Guatemalan Embassy in the UK","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/foreign-embassies-in-the-uk"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to extend your visa or permit to stay, you must apply to the Institute of Migration (Spanish language website)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you get your passport stamped on entry."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you do not get your passport stamped when you arrive, you will get a fine and be delayed on your departure. If you notice your passport has not been stamped, return to the immigration desk immediately, or go to the Institute of Migration (in Spanish) offices in Guatemala City as soon as possible."]},{"k":"h","s":["Immigration declaration form"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you enter or exit Guatemala, you must ",["complete the online immigration declaration form","https://farm2.sat.gob.gt/declaDelViajeroGt-web/pages/public/declaracionJuradaDelViajero.html"],". If you can, print the confirmation email you receive and carry it with you. Alternatively take a screenshot of the email QR code. If you need more information, contact your travel agent or the ",["Institute of Migration","https://igm.gob.gt/protocolo-sanitario-de-ingreso-a-guatemala/"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"p","s":["This form can be done on arrival just before immigration, there are dedicated terminals to complete the online form. However, this can delay your arrival into the country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Airport tax"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must pay airport departure tax of 30 US dollars (or the same amount in Guatemalan quetzal). This is normally included in the price of your ticket."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling with children"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter or exit Guatemala, children aged 17 and under travelling alone or with only one parent or legal guardian must have a notarised letter in Spanish from the parents or guardians not travelling with them. Contact the ",["Guatemalan Embassy","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/foreign-embassies-in-the-uk"],", or if you’re in Guatemala the nearest ",["Institute of Migration","https://igm.gob.gt/"]," (Spanish language website), for information about what documents you need. Single parents will need additional documents."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling between Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras and Nicaragua"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can travel between Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras and Nicaragua for up to 90 days without a visa. Read the travel advice for:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["El Salvador","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/el-salvador/entry-requirements"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Honduras","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/honduras/entry-requirements"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Nicaragua","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/nicaragua/entry-requirements"]]},{"k":"p","s":["The 90-day period starts when you enter any of these countries and does not restart when you cross borders. You can extend your stay by up to 30 days by paying a fee before the 90 days expires. If you overstay, you may get a fine."]},{"k":"p","s":["You’ll have to go through immigration checks at borders. If you’re expelled from one of these countries, you’re also excluded from the other 3."]},{"k":"h","s":["Illegal entry fees at land borders"]},{"k":"p","s":["Guatemalan customs or immigration officials sometimes ask travellers crossing land borders to pay an ‘entry fee’. This is illegal. If you are asked to pay a fee, ask for an official receipt, and you may find the officials drop the request."]},{"k":"p","s":["When crossing into Guatemala by land, be wary of people offering to help process your entry into the country. They may be trying to overcharge you for unnecessary services."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Guatemala, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Guatemala guide","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country-details.php?cnt=96#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Guatemala. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Taking money into Guatemala"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is often not possible to exchange British pounds in Guatemala. It is much easier to exchange US dollars."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Guatemala","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Guatemala"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Guatemala, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Incidents of political violence, strikes and large demonstrations can occur, often with little or no notice. Most demonstrations are peaceful, but they can turn violent. Protestors may block roads and public facilities, including the international airport, without notice."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Guatemala has one of the highest violent crime rates in Latin America. Although most of the serious crime involves local gangs, you could be caught up in incidents as they can happen in tourist areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["No parts of Guatemala City are free from crime, including Zone 10 (Zona Viva), which is popular with tourists and foreign residents. Take care in Zone 1 where tourists have been targeted by opportunistic criminals, this is the historical centre where cheaper hotels are located and several bus routes end."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to visit or stay in the outer zones of the City such as Zones 18-25, you should use reputable chain hotels and research the area extensively as these zones are known for higher levels of crime. Zones 6, 7 and 8 should also be treated with caution."]},{"k":"p","s":["When travelling to more rural parts of Guatemala you should travel with a reputable tour company and follow local security advice. Areas in and around the departments of Huehuetenango, Chiquimula, Zacapa, Izabal, San Marcos and Petén experience high levels of crime. You should remain within the touristic areas and travel during daylight hours where possible."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Violent crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Violent attacks, including sexual assault and gun crime, can take place anywhere and at any time of the day. Attackers have killed and injured victims who resisted. There is a low arrest and conviction rate."]},{"k":"p","s":["Carjacking and armed robbery are common."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been armed attacks or robberies on most tourist routes between Guatemala City, Antigua, Tikal, Petén and Lake Atitlán. See ",["Regional risks","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can take precautions such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["not displaying valuables like laptops, cameras and mobile phones"]},{"k":"li","s":["not wearing a lot of jewellery"]},{"k":"li","s":["carrying only small amounts of cash – avoid withdrawing a lot of money, particularly at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping valuables safe (for example, in a hotel safe)"]},{"k":"li","s":["not travelling alone or at night, especially near borders or in areas without many people"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you go to remote areas, it may be safer to travel with others or a reputable tour company."]},{"k":"p","s":["For shorter trips within towns and cities the safest option is to take radio-dispatched taxis (which are usually yellow), Uber (where available) or hotel taxis. You can buy pre-paid taxi vouchers from the office of ",["INGUAT, Guatemala’s tourist agency","https://inguat.gob.gt/"]," (Spanish language website) in the airport’s arrivals terminal."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re driving, it is generally safer to travel on main roads. There is a greater risk of attack by gangs on quieter routes. Travel in convoy if possible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be aware of your surroundings and take care around ATMs, petrol station forecourts, the airport, bus stations and shopping centres."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can get up-to-date security information from INGUAT."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a victim of a criminal attack, INGUAT has a tourist assistance and emergency service section called PROATUR who can help accompany you to your hotel or other safe place. See ",["Getting help","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/getting-help"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Buses and coaches"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid travelling on public buses (repainted US school buses). There have been incidents of attacks and extortion by local gangs on bus drivers resulting in serious injury or death. These buses are usually poorly maintained, overcrowded and do not generally adhere to the rules of the road, driving at dangerous speeds on perilous roads. Accidents are common and sometimes fatal."]},{"k":"p","s":["Private intercity coach services are generally safer, but gangs have also targeted these, even on main roads."]},{"k":"hs","s":["ATM tampering"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check ATMs for evidence of tampering. Affected machines may not be easy to spot. It’s safer to change money in hotels, at banks or at foreign exchange offices."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Fake police officers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals posing as police officers have committed theft, extortion and sexual assault in Guatemala."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scams"]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign visitors are at risk of scams. Scams come in many forms and can lead to great financial loss. Warn your friends and family to be sceptical if they are asked to transfer funds to you in Guatemala. Tell them to contact you to check that you have made this request."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal kidnapping"]},{"k":"p","s":["Express kidnappings – short term opportunistic abductions, aimed at extracting cash from the victim – are common in Guatemala. Victims can be targeted or selected at random. They are held while criminals withdraw money from their bank accounts using their cards. Many cases involve victims that have been picked up by taxis (registered or illegal) hailed from the street. Be aware of your surroundings and always let someone you trust know your whereabouts, especially after dark."]},{"k":"p","s":["Business people and visitors perceived to be wealthy may be targeted by kidnappers in search of payouts. Be discreet about discussing your financial or business affairs in places where you may be overheard by others, and when arranging meetings use a secure location."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a victim of kidnapping, follow the kidnapper’s instructions."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is a legal requirement to always carry ID. In most parts of the country, you can carry a copy of your passport’s photo page for identification purposes. In San Pedro La Laguna, Sololá, local authorities may not accept a copy and may fine or detain you if you cannot show your original passport or a certified copy. Always co-operate with military and police officers and be prepared for checkpoints."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws and bans"]},{"k":"p","s":["During specific periods (such as elections) alcohol sales may be banned or restricted during certain times."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are severe penalties for drug trafficking and the use of drugs. Guatemalan prisons are overcrowded, violent, and dirty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking photos without permission"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not take photographs without permission, especially of children. This is particularly important in more remote areas such as Quiché, Petén, San Marcos and Chiquimula provinces. There have been attacks related to accusations and fears of child kidnapping for adoption or theft of vital organs. Foreigners have been caught up in the violence. You may need to pay a small amount of money to take photographs of both children and adults."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same sex sexual activity is not illegal, although there are no laws guaranteeing freedom from discrimination on the grounds of sexual orientation. Same-sex marriages are not recognised in Guatemala. In Guatemala City, local people are largely tolerant of LGBT+ people. Outside Guatemala City, attitudes are more conservative. Showing affection in public may bring verbal and physical attacks, harassment and discrimination."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Climbing volcanoes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you climb volcanoes, visit the websites of the Guatemalan Meteorological Office (Spanish language website) and ",["CONRED, Guatemala’s disaster agency","https://conred.gob.gt/"]," for information on access, restrictions and recommendations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow the advice of local authorities. Some volcanoes are at high altitude with freezing temperatures at night. Warm clothing and waterproofs are essential. Local tour organisers tend to underestimate the risks and deaths have occurred. There is no mountain rescue service."]},{"k":"p","s":["See more information on ",["Volunteering and adventure travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Guatemala for up to 3 months. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving rules and safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Guatemala:"]},{"k":"li","s":["it is illegal to use a mobile phone while driving"]},{"k":"li","s":["speed limits are enforced"]},{"k":"p","s":["Randomly located police checkpoints can be setup around the city and drivers are usually pulled over for documentation and outstanding penalty checks. Ensure you have your licence, ID and car rental or ownership paperwork to hand."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence. If you are tested and found to have any alcohol in your system, you may get a fine, your licence confiscated and possible imprisonment. However, drinking-driving is common in Guatemala."]},{"k":"p","s":["In more isolated locations, roads are unpaved and you may need a 4-wheel drive vehicle."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in an accident, contact the National Police (telephone: 110) or the fire brigade (telephone: 122 or 123) and wait for them to arrive. PROVIAL (telephone: +502 2419 2121 or 1520), a roadside assistance force, patrols most of the major roads in the country. However, patrols are infrequent."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information on road laws, see ",["Guatemala Department of Transport","https://transito.gob.gt/legislacion-en-materia-de-transito/"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Intercity buses"]},{"k":"p","s":["Guatemala Municipality no longer allows some intercity buses to enter the city centre. They drop passengers at various points on the city outskirts."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rainy season"]},{"k":"p","s":["The rainy season in Guatemala normally runs from May to November, the same time as the hurricane season in the Caribbean. Heavy rains cause frequent flooding and landslides, and poorly maintained roads and bridges have been known to collapse."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check local media and consult your tour operator and PROATUR, Guatemala’s tourist assistance service (see ",["Getting help","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/getting-help"],"). Travel routes are likely to face disruption. Monitor local and international weather updates from the World Meteorological Organization and the US National Hurricane Center. Follow the advice of the local authorities, including any evacuation orders."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are frequent minor earth tremors and there is a risk of earthquakes in Guatemala. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"]," or tsunami."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volcanic eruptions"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of volcanic eruptions in Guatemala. Monitor local media and seek advice from your tour operator in case of possible travel disruption. For further information see ",["CONRED, Guatemala’s disaster agency","https://conred.gob.gt/"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Guatemala. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Borders with Mexico, Honduras, El Salvador and Belize"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel within 5km of the Mexican border from the Pacific Coast up to and including the Gracias a Dios crossing. This is due to increased and sustained gang-related violence along the Mexico-Guatemala border."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO also advises against all but essential travel to the towns of Santa Ana Huista, San Antonio Huista and La Democracia in the department of Huehuetenango"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pay particular attention to your security in the border areas with Mexico, Honduras, El Salvador and Belize."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take care near the Belize-Guatemala border because of the ongoing dispute between the 2 countries. Use only officially recognised border crossings."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ixchiguan and Tajumulco"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of violent crime in the municipalities of Ixchiguan and Tajumulco in the department of San Marcos. If you plan to travel to these areas, get advice from PROATUR, Guatemala’s tourist assistance service (see ",["Getting help","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/getting-help"]," for contact details), including whether current conditions mean it is safe to climb the Tajumulco volcano."]},{"k":"h","s":["Roads around Sololá, Panajachel and Lake Atitlán"]},{"k":"p","s":["PROATUR, Guatemala’s tourist assistance service (see ",["Getting help","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/getting-help"]," for contact details) gives advice on routes when travelling in and around Sololá, Panajachel and Lake Atitlán. PROATUR advises:"]},{"k":"li","s":["to avoid the Godinez bypass between Guatemala City and Panajachel (which passes through Patzún) which is an isolated area with high risks of criminality - the Pan American Highway is an alternative route that will take you from Guatemala City to Sololá avoiding these areas."]},{"k":"li","s":["to avoid the road between Cocales (Suchitepequez) and San Lucas Toliman (Atitlán) if possible, because of the risks of criminality and poor road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Boat services between towns on the shore of Lake Atitlán may be a safer alternative."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 122 or 123 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Guatemala","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/96/guatemala"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Guatemala","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/96/guatemala"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Guatemala. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS has information on ",["whether you can take your medicine abroad","https://www.nhs.uk/common-health-questions/medicines/can-i-take-my-medicine-abroad/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot use British-issued prescriptions in Guatemala. To find a pharmacy (‘farmacia’) look for one of the big national chains such as Meykos, Cruz Verde, Galeno or Batres."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare facilities in Guatemala"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["English-speaking doctors in Guatemala","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/list-of-medical-facilities-in-guatemala-city"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Guatemala"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire and Ambulance: 122 or 123"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 110"]},{"k":"h","s":["PROATUR, Guatemala tourist assistance"]},{"k":"p","s":["PROATUR, Guatemala’s tourist assistance service, provides 24-hour emergency assistance and routine guidance to tourists (call centre staff speak English and Spanish):"]},{"k":"li","s":["Telephone: +502 2290 2810 or 1500 if dialling from inside Guatemala"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Guatemala"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Guatemala"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Guatemala"]," in Guatemala"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Guatemala","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/information-sheet-relating-to-deaths-in-guatemala"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Guatemala","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/prisoners-pack-for-guatemala"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/guatemala/british-embassy-guatemala-city"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guatemala/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Guatemala","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-guatemala"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":23244,"change":"New information about a State of Emergency in Zacapa and Izabal ('Warnings and insurance' page)."},"https://theconversation.com/what-makes-food-organic-for-many-consumers-the-answer-isnt-always-on-the-label-293818":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"f28af7d7fa73dedf329e2e4b17998449cbf0da9950fa84e043b21d8dfca1de15","read":"https://theconversation.com/what-makes-food-organic-for-many-consumers-the-answer-isnt-always-on-the-label-293818","bytes":177646,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"What makes food ‘organic’? For many consumers, the answer isn’t always on the label","by":[{"n":"Xiaoxi Zhu","r":"Doctoral Candidate, Faculty of Business and Economics, University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau"}],"pub":"2026-11-15T17:27:08Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["“Organic” is a word we encounter every time we visit the supermarket. It’s also one that can mean very different things to different people."]},{"k":"p","s":["Surveys show that taste, quality, environmental concerns and perceived health and safety benefits all influence consumers’ decisions to buy ",["organic food","https://theconversation.com/topics/organic-food-2988"],". But less is known about how people interpret “organic” and incorporate it into their everyday lives."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drawing on videos recorded by consumers themselves, ",["my new research","https://www.emerald.com/qmr/article-abstract/doi/10.1108/QMR-01-2025-0014/1395181/Consumer-curated-post-purchase-video-a?redirectedFrom=fulltext"]," explores how these ideas influence people’s relationships with food."]},{"k":"p","s":["It shows how the search for food they can trust can lead shoppers from supermarkets to growers and their own gardens, with important lessons for producers, retailers and researchers."]},{"k":"h","s":["Looking past the organic label"]},{"k":"p","s":["As in other countries, organic food in New Zealand is big business. Organics Aotearoa New Zealand’s ",["2025 market report","https://www.oanz.org/nzmarket-report-2025"]," showed the national organic sector to be worth NZ$1.18 billion in 2024."]},{"k":"p","s":["While a ",["2020 government-led consumer survey","https://www.mpi.govt.nz/dmsdocument/43198/direct"]," found price was a barrier for most respondents who didn’t buy organic products, around three-quarters of all respondents still reported buying organic food or drink. So, what exactly are they paying for?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Organic agriculture is generally understood as a system that supports the health of soils, ecosystems and people. ",["Certification","https://www.mpi.govt.nz/agriculture/organic-product-requirements-in-nz"]," helps ensure organic products meet recognised standards throughout the supply chain."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet my research highlighted that consumers don’t necessarily define organic food by what’s on the label."]},{"k":"p","s":["Across three related studies, I drew on the experiences of 33 Chinese consumers of organic food, examining how trust, personal values and migration experiences shaped their food practices. While not representative of all Chinese or New Zealand consumers, the findings offer insights into how people practise organic living."]},{"k":"p","s":["During the COVID-19 pandemic, restrictions made it difficult to observe participants’ routines. So I asked participants in one of the studies to record short videos showing their food and how they obtained it."]},{"k":"p","s":["They could film their groceries, unpack a delivery or take the camera into their garden. I then used their videos to guide interviews, asking why they had chosen to show particular things."]},{"k":"p","s":["The recordings highlighted how organic consumption could involve growing and sharing food as well as buying it. Some participants showed vegetables growing at home, while others presented produce from a community garden."]},{"k":"p","s":["One person filmed her supermarket shopping. After returning to China from studying in the United States, she began buying organic products in Shanghai. Later, she started making tofu, growing bean sprouts and harvesting vegetables on her balcony."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another person unpacked a box of produce, explaining how he’d asked the supplier to pack it tightly and use less plastic to reduce waste. Both examples showed how people’s interest in organic food influenced their everyday habits in ways that simply asking about their shopping might not have uncovered."]},{"k":"p","s":["The videos also challenged some assumptions. One participant described himself as an enthusiastic organic consumer but showed expensive supermarket products without organic labels."]},{"k":"p","s":["Further discussion revealed that he’d linked organic food with premium prices, luxury packaging and upmarket shops, rather than certification or production methods. His understanding differed markedly from the researchers’ definition of organic food."]},{"k":"p","s":["The videos were selective accounts of everyday life, with participants deciding what to show. But examining those choices alongside interviews helped explain why particular products and activities felt important to them."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why trust is key"]},{"k":"p","s":["Trust emerged as a central theme across my research – and it’s also a concern for shoppers more generally. In the 2020 survey, for instance, 21% of New Zealand respondents who didn’t buy organic products cited a lack of trust in organic claims as a barrier."]},{"k":"p","s":["While certain participants in my research relied on certification, others placed greater faith in personal recommendations, community networks or direct relationships with growers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Doubts about conventional food systems sometimes extended to organic claims, prompting people to change their food sources or grow and prepare more themselves. Consumers could share a desire for trustworthy food while holding very different ideas about what made it trustworthy."]},{"k":"p","s":["For producers and retailers, the findings reinforce the need for clear information about where organic food comes from, how it is produced and how its claims are verified. For researchers, they demonstrate the value of videos and interviews in exploring consumers’ habits and attitudes beyond their purchasing decisions."]},{"k":"p","s":["Someone who grows more of their own food might buy less from an organic retailer, all while becoming more committed to the values they associate with organic living."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ultimately, what people do with food, what they value and whom they trust can tell us just as much about their choices as what they put in their shopping trolley."]}],"chars":5012},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/slovenia":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"886cdbf306b5becfe755db113ffaa9a6d9b2bb9a730735889bdc089175b92b4f","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/slovenia","bytes":76454,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Slovenia travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-08T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-11T10:22:42+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/slovenia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Slovenia set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Slovenian Embassy in the UK","https://www.gov.si/en/representations/embassy-london/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Slovenia follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["travel without a visa to the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/travel-to-eu-schengen-area"],", which includes Slovenia, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["requirements for working in Slovenia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-slovenia-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day visa-free limit. Visits to Schengen countries in the 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days. If you overstay the 90-day visa-free limit, you may have to pay an on-the-spot fine. If you cannot pay the fine, the border police can confiscate your documents or assets until you pay. You may also be banned from entering Schengen countries for up to 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["At Slovenian border control, you may need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove that you have enough money for your stay"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, you must meet the Slovenian government’s entry requirements. Check which type of visa or permit you need with the ",["Slovenian Embassy in the UK","https://www.gov.si/en/representations/embassy-london/visa-information-of-the-embassy-london/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in Slovenia with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about ",["entry processes if you live in Slovenia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-slovenia#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Checks at land borders"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are checks at Slovenia’s land borders with:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Italy"]},{"k":"li","s":["Austria"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are travelling from Croatia or Hungary, only enter Slovenia at ",["border crossings open to all passengers","https://www.gov.si/en/registries/projects/zacasni-nadzor-na-notranji-meji/"],". Allow extra time for crossing Slovenia’s land borders and be ready to show your passport or residency card. Checks may also involve vehicle inspections."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the police"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must register with the police within 3 days of your arrival, but any registered hotel or guest house will do this for you. If you are staying in self-catering accommodation, or with friends or family, check registration arrangements with your booking agent or your host. If you do not register your presence in Slovenia, you could get a fine."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Slovenia guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/198/slovenia#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Slovenia","https://www.fu.gov.si/en/customs/areas_of_work/import_of_goods/"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food into Slovenia"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EU countries","https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animalproducts/personal_imports_en"],". There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Slovenia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 euros or more. You will get a certified declaration to show you brought it in with you. If you do not, your money could be seized when you leave."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Slovenia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Slovenia cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry a copy of your passport as a form of ID. You can get a 50 to 200 euro fine if the police ask you for ID and you cannot show it."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, carry severe penalties. You should expect a long jail sentence and heavy fines for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Slovenia have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road crossing fines"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are heavy fines for not using designated road crossing points (‘jaywalking’). If you cannot pay an on-the-spot fine, the police or authorities can confiscate your documents or assets until you pay."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tour guides"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tour guides must display a tour guide card to show they are registered with the Slovenian authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Emergency siren tests"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Slovenian authorities test a nationwide emergency siren on the first Saturday of every month at 12pm. If you hear the siren at another time, follow instructions from the authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Skiing and mountaineering"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning a skiing or mountaineering holiday, see ",["Policija - Helpful Advice to all Hikers, Trekkers and Mountaineers","https://www.policija.si/eng/prevention/safety-on-holidays/safe-mountaineering/helpful-advice-to-all-hikers-trekkers-and-mountaineers"]," and ",["National Meteorological Service of Slovenia","https://meteo.arso.gov.si/met/en/"]," before you travel. Off-piste skiing is highly dangerous as there is a risk of avalanches in some areas. See ",["Preparing for winter sports abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/winter-sports-stay-safe-on-the-slopes"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to hike in Slovenia:"]},{"k":"li","s":["take particular care if you’re in an area where bears have been sighted. Visitors in mountain areas occasionally encounter bears and wolves"]},{"k":"li","s":["consider the level of difficulty and distance"]},{"k":"li","s":["plan for the weakest member of your group"]},{"k":"li","s":["use a printed or digital map with marked paths and their descriptions"]},{"k":"li","s":["tell family or friends of your plans"]},{"k":"li","s":["check whether mountain cabins are open"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not use hiking trails that have been closed or take shortcuts"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bears are present in wooded areas throughout Slovenia, including near cities. In 2025 a bear attacked a person walking their dog in Skofljica, 6 miles southeast of Ljubljana."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some demanding mountain trails can include sections of via ferrata (steel wires and metal rungs), which need a climbing harness and helmet."]},{"k":"p","s":["For mountain rescue, call 112."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Wild camping"]},{"k":"p","s":["Wild camping is illegal in Slovenia. Only camp in registered campsites, or if you are travelling with a camper van or motorhome in a designated camper stop (“Parking za avtodome”, also known as “PZA”). Fines for illegal camping depend upon the local municipality or authority and can range from 40 to 500 euros. Enforcement is especially strict in national parks, including Triglav National Park."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Slovenia, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Slovenia guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/slovenia/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of, such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["always driving with headlights on"]},{"k":"li","s":["carrying a warning triangle, reflective jacket and first aid kit"]},{"k":"li","s":["on-the-spot fines for offences including speeding, drink-driving and using a mobile phone without a wireless headset"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can drive in Slovenia on your UK driving licence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a higher minimum age."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you live in Slovenia, check the ",["driving requirements for residents","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-slovenia#driving-in-slovenia"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check if you need a ",["UK sticker to drive your car outside the UK","https://www.gov.uk/displaying-number-plates/flags-identifiers-and-stickers"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Slovenia. The blood alcohol limit for experienced drivers is 0.05% (in England it is 0.08%). If you are under 21 or have had your licence less than 2 years, it is illegal to have any alcohol in your system. You could get a fine and possible imprisonment if you’re caught over the limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["Slovenia traffic and border crossing reports","https://www.promet.si/en"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["If your vehicle breaks down on a motorway or expressway:"]},{"k":"li","s":["call 1970 (or +386 1 518 8 518) for assistance"]},{"k":"li","s":["turn on your hazard lights"]},{"k":"li","s":["ensure all passengers wear reflective jackets"]},{"k":"li","s":["leave the vehicle safely and place a warning triangle behind it"]},{"k":"li","s":["wait behind the safety barrier where possible"]},{"k":"li","s":["in tunnels, leave in the direction of the entrance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Vehicles stopped in a traffic lane, slip road, or tunnel emergency bay may be removed by a motorway operator-contracted towing service. If your vehicle is on the hard shoulder, an emergency bay or a rest area, you can arrange your own recovery, but it must normally be moved within 2 hours."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be aware that unauthorised recovery operators may offer assistance and charge high fees. UUse only authorised recovery providers and, if in doubt, call 1970 for advice. Authorised providers display a DARS logo on their vehicle."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Motorway tolls"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you drive on Slovenian motorways, you must buy an ‘e-vignette’ for electronic tolls. You can buy weekly, monthly or yearly e-vignettes at:"]},{"k":"li","s":["petrol stations"]},{"k":"li","s":["post offices"]},{"k":"li","s":["DARS (the Slovenian Motorway Company) offices in Slovenia and in neighbouring countries near the border"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can also ",["buy e-vignettes from DARS online","https://www.dars.si/"]," before or during your stay in Slovenia. Only use the official DARS website to buy these online."]},{"k":"p","s":["If your vehicle weighs more than 3.5 tonnes, you must register it under the ",["DarsGo system","https://www.darsgo.si/portal/en/home"]," to use Slovenia’s motorways and expressways. Collect a DarsGo unit from a DarsGo service point when you enter Slovenia for the first time."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving in winter"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must use winter equipment from 15 November until 15 March and whenever there are winter weather conditions (for example, snowfall, blizzards or black ice)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Private cars and vehicles up to 3.5 tonnes must have winter tyres on all 4 wheels, or summer tyres on all 4 wheels and snow chains in the car boot. The minimum tyre tread depth is 3mm."]},{"k":"p","s":["Vehicles over 3.5 tonnes must have winter tyres on the driving wheels or summer tyres on all 4 wheels and snow chains in the boot."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can get a fine of 125 euros for not having this equipment, or 417 euros if you also cause road delays."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["weather forecasts and warnings in English","https://www.meteoalarm.org/en/live/?s=slovenia"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Seasonal storms"]},{"k":"p","s":["All parts of Slovenia can experience sudden, extreme storms. There can be strong winds and rain, snowfall, lightning and powerful hailstorms. Storms can cause landslides and road closures. Check weather and traffic reports before you travel. If you’re caught in a storm, find shelter until the storm passes and follow local advice."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["Flash floods can be dangerous, happen with little warning and disrupt transport and other networks. Keep a close eye on weather forecasts and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Wildfires"]},{"k":"p","s":["During especially hot and dry periods there is a risk of wildfires. It’s illegal to do anything that starts a fire that poses a danger to human life or could cause significant damage."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Western Slovenia is on an earthquake fault line and is subject to occasional tremors. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Slovenia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/198/slovenia#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Slovenia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/198/slovenia"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["tick bites and tick-borne encephalitis","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/38/insect-and-tick-bite-avoidance"]]},{"k":"li","s":["poor air quality in parts of Slovenia at certain times of the year"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Slovenia. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Slovenia"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Slovenia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/slovenia-list-of-medical-facilities"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Slovenia, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Slovenia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-slovenia#healthcare"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Slovenia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 113"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance, fire and mountain rescue: 112"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=slovenia"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=slovenia"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Slovenia"]," in Slovenia"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Slovenia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-information-for-slovenia"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Slovenia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/slovenia-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/slovenia/british-embassy-ljubljana"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/slovenia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Slovenia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-slovenia"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":19058,"change":"Updated information on European Entry-Exit System (EES) (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/grattan-on-friday-almost-a-year-after-bondi-is-our-social-cohesion-a-boiling-frog-296272":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-19T07:53Z","sha":"707829736b7081434a4aaf21b3e8f32fd22247de6a99fb69fb6f31db40680f13","read":"https://theconversation.com/grattan-on-friday-almost-a-year-after-bondi-is-our-social-cohesion-a-boiling-frog-296272","bytes":178812,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Grattan on Friday: Almost a year after Bondi, is our social cohesion a boiling frog?","by":[{"n":"Michelle Grattan","r":"Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra"}],"pub":"2026-11-19T07:08:31Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Next month, Australia will relive the trauma of the Bondi massacre, with the anniversary of the December 14 shootings and the release days later of the report from the ",["Royal Commission on Antisemitism and Social Cohesion","https://asc.royalcommission.gov.au/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The report will provide a snapshot (albeit partial) of Australia’s social health. It will be a time of reckoning for the nation, a time to consider the question: has our social cohesion become a boiling frog?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Commissioner Virginia Bell, a former High Court judge, will home in on security issues, as well as documenting the appalling antisemitism that erupted in this country, not just the attacks and other incidents themselves, but also the testimony given by so many witnesses. She will also likely highlight how institutions, notably the universities, fell short in dealing with the antisemitism crisis."]},{"k":"p","s":["The commission received 20,203 submissions, heard 348 witnesses, and attracted 151,800 livestream views."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bell didn’t take evidence from the prime minister or any other ministers. She didn’t ask them to appear and they didn’t offer. Given the policy and practical matters examined, it is strange that Home Affairs Minister Tony Burke wasn’t called. ASIO and Australian Federal Police officials appeared, and both are under Burke’s umbrella. One might have expected Bell would have wanted to hear from the relevant minister about his interactions with them."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ahead of the commission’s recommendations, Jewish leaders have made new pitches for extra measures to fight antisemitism."]},{"k":"p","s":["The special envoy to combat antisemitism, Jillian Segal, has updated her earlier ",["report’s recommendations","https://www.aseca.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-07/2025-aseca-plan.pdf"],", including urging the transfer of the responsibilities of the ABC and SBS ombudsmen to an external ombudsman. The government has quickly ruled this out."]},{"k":"p","s":["The antisemitism issue is moving into the debate over the government’s digital duty of care legislation, now before parliament. A coalition of Jewish peak organisations is arguing the legislation’s definition of “seriously harmful material” should be expanded to that involving antisemitism (among other things) and that there should be a pathway for individuals or groups to seek redress without facing enormous court costs."]},{"k":"p","s":["Peter Wertheim, co-CEO of the Executive Council of Australian Jewry, told a Senate committee last week that missing from the definition “is content that attacks groups on the basis of an inherent attribute or characteristic: such as, sexual orientation, gender identity, race and disability”."]},{"k":"p","s":["“The consequence is that the duty, as currently drafted, does not protect against the vast majority of racist, misogynist or antisemitic hate speech online, which is among the most virulent drivers of contemporary hatred,” he said."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wertheim ran into pushback from Opposition communications spokeswoman Sarah Henderson, on the grounds that the legislation’s power would be open to potential abuse by a government."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wertheim countered: “I would rather see that power in the hands of a democratically elected and accountable government than in the hands of unaccountable tech companies, as it is at the moment, if I had to make a choice”."]},{"k":"p","s":["The issues around social cohesion generally are by their nature more amorphous than those relating to security and even antisemitism specifically."]},{"k":"p","s":["While Bell’s focus has been antisemitism, one would hope, as an observer of the commission neatly puts it, that her report “will open doors through which others can pass” to identify and grapple with the deepening problems facing social cohesion and, by extension, the mounting challenges of managing multiculturalism."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Scanlon Index tracks core indicators to measure cohesion. In its ",["Mapping Social Cohesion 2026 report","https://scanloninstitute.org.au/mapping-social-cohesion-2026/"],", based on a survey of 10,000 people and released this month, the index remained stable, at a score of 78. But below the surface, the picture was different."]},{"k":"p","s":["Three-quarters of participants agreed “multiculturalism has been good for Australia”. This is a strong number – but in 2023 it was 89%. The proportion saying migrants improve Australian society has gone from 85% in 2023 to 70%. And those thinking “too many migrants are not adopting Australian values” has jumped from 52% to 66%."]},{"k":"p","s":["“People are increasingly likely to express negative attitudes towards Muslim and Jewish people, as well as immigrants from Asia and Africa,” the report says. Negativity towards Muslims and Jews has increased noticeably in the wake of the Middle East conflicts."]},{"k":"p","s":["“Australia’s multicultural fabric is strained, though recognition of immigrants and their capacity to contribute to Australian society remain high.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["To change the metaphor, while the temperature in the multicultural pot is still at tolerable levels, it is definitely rising and it could endanger the frog."]},{"k":"p","s":["The strains are mixed with, and worsened by, other issues and attitudes. “Many people continue to face financial stress and hardship in ways that are likely harmful to social cohesion,” the Scanlon report notes."]},{"k":"p","s":["And, crucially, there is the issue of trust. “Distrust in government and other institutions is growing and is increasingly polarised along political lines.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Scanlon figures are stark. The proportion of adults who think the government can be trusted to “do the right thing for the Australian people” has declined from 54% in 2020 to 31% in 2026, while the proportion who say they almost never trust the government increased from 6% to 22%."]},{"k":"p","s":["The surge in support for One Nation, which took off after Bondi and surfed the issue of high immigration, is obviously relevant in any debate about social cohesion. The growth in that party’s support can be seen as both a product of and a contributor to a decline in cohesion. There’s plenty of irony here: One Nation laments a loss of cohesion but plays a role in that loss."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Scanlon report argues we can best strengthen social cohesion by focusing on “personal and social wellbeing, respect, empathy and out common humanity, rather than manufacturing sameness”."]},{"k":"p","s":["In building or repairing cohesion, the local is important. Allegra Spender, crossbench member for Wentworth, which takes in Bondi, ",["told the The Conversation politics podcast","https://theconversation.com/politics-with-michelle-grattan-allegra-spender-on-community-anger-and-politicians-bad-behaviour-296820"]," this week she has brought together principals and students from all of the schools in the electorate – those of faith and no faith. The reason: “So that kids can get to know each other firstly as people before we start to make assumptions about other people based on whatever labels others give to them”."]},{"k":"p","s":["Spender wants to see recommendations from the royal commission “about fighting antisemitism in institutions, but also about building the understanding and connections between different parts of our community”."]},{"k":"p","s":["In other words, as Spender asks, how do we develop a new sort of “inclusive patriotism” based on the values that unite us?"]}],"chars":6752}};
